-- Q1
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 72-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and hypertension attends his GP for an annual diabetic eye screening result review. The screening report states: "R2 — moderate non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy, no maculopathy. L1 — mild NPDR." He has no visual symptoms. His most recent HbA1c is 74 mmol/mol and blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg. According to NICE guidance on diabetic eye disease, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology within 2 weeks as R2 retinopathy requires immediate laser treatment',
'Reassure that R2 retinopathy is benign; continue annual screening and review diabetic control',
'Optimise glycaemic control and blood pressure; refer routinely to hospital eye service for R2 retinopathy assessment; continue annual screening',
'Refer immediately to the emergency eye unit as moderate NPDR carries high risk of acute vitreous haemorrhage',
'Discharge from diabetic screening as he has no visual symptoms',
'C',
'CORRECT. R2 moderate non-proliferative diabetic retinopathy without maculopathy requires routine referral to the hospital eye service for ophthalmological assessment. This is not an emergency — R3 proliferative retinopathy or maculopathy would require urgent or fast-track referral. Critically, the GP must address the modifiable risk factors driving progression: HbA1c of 74 mmol/mol indicates suboptimal glycaemic control (target <53 mmol/mol in most patients with established complications), and BP 158/96 exceeds the target of <130/80 mmHg for people with diabetes and retinopathy per NICE. Poor glycaemic and BP control are the two strongest modifiable risk factors for diabetic retinopathy progression. Optimising these in primary care is as important as the specialist referral. NICE',
'Incorrect. R2 retinopathy does not require emergency or 2-week referral. Urgent referral is indicated for R3 (proliferative DR), maculopathy, or sudden visual loss. Laser treatment is not initiated without full ophthalmological assessment. Routine referral to the hospital eye service is appropriate for R2.',
'Incorrect. R2 retinopathy is not benign — it represents moderate NPDR which carries significant risk of progression to sight-threatening disease if risk factors are not optimised. Simply continuing annual screening without routine ophthalmology referral and without optimising HbA1c and BP is inadequate management.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Acute vitreous haemorrhage is a complication of proliferative diabetic retinopathy (R3) — not moderate NPDR (R2). Emergency eye unit referral is not indicated at this stage. The patient has no visual symptoms and no proliferative changes.',
'Incorrect. R2 retinopathy requires ongoing surveillance and referral — it is not an indication for discharge from screening. Diabetic eye screening should continue annually (or more frequently if clinically indicated). Discharge would leave the patient without appropriate monitoring for a potentially sight-threatening condition.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q2
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 68-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 3-hour history of sudden painless loss of vision in her right eye. She describes the vision loss as "a curtain coming down from above." She has a history of hypertension, hyperlipidaemia, and paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (on apixaban). On examination, visual acuity in the right eye is counting fingers at 1 metre. The left eye is unaffected. Her blood pressure is 172/98 mmHg. According to NICE guidance on sudden visual loss, what is the most appropriate immediate management?',
'Arrange urgent ophthalmology review within 1 week and optimise her blood pressure in primary care',
'Arrange same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment; this presentation is consistent with retinal detachment or central retinal artery/vein occlusion requiring urgent specialist evaluation',
'Reassure her that painless visual loss in older patients is commonly caused by vitreous detachment which resolves spontaneously',
'Refer to neurology for urgent MRI brain to exclude posterior cortical stroke before ophthalmology assessment',
'Prescribe aspirin 300 mg immediately and arrange urgent echocardiography for cardioembolic source',
'B',
'CORRECT. Sudden painless unilateral visual loss is an ophthalmic emergency requiring same-day assessment. The curtain-like descending visual loss from above is classic for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (fluid accumulates under the retina, with the curtain advancing as more retina detaches). Alternative diagnoses include central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO — sudden complete visual loss, cherry red spot), central retinal vein occlusion (CRVO — flame haemorrhages), or vitreous haemorrhage. All require same-day ophthalmological assessment. In the case of retinal detachment, time to treatment is critical — macula-off detachments have significantly worse visual outcomes. The GP should arrange immediate transfer to the emergency eye unit. NICE',
'Incorrect. A 1-week ophthalmology review is wholly inadequate for sudden complete unilateral visual loss. Retinal detachment, CRAO, and CRVO require same-day emergency assessment. A 1-week delay could result in permanent blindness from an untreated retinal detachment or missed thromboembolic event.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. The curtain-like descending field loss is a classic symptom of retinal detachment, not vitreous detachment. Posterior vitreous detachment (PVD) causes floaters and flashes of light but does NOT cause a dense curtain-like field defect with visual acuity reduced to counting fingers. Reassurance without urgent referral would be negligent.',
'Incorrect. While posterior cortical stroke can cause visual loss, the presentation here — monocular visual loss with a curtain-like descending field defect reducing VA to counting fingers — points to an intraocular cause (retinal detachment, CRAO/CRVO). Cortical strokes cause homonymous hemianopia affecting both eyes in the same visual field. Same-day ophthalmology assessment should not be delayed by neurological investigation.',
'Incorrect. While aspirin and cardioembolic investigation may be relevant if CRAO is confirmed, prescribing aspirin before diagnosis is established is premature. She is already on apixaban for AF. The priority is same-day ophthalmological assessment — the specific management will depend on the diagnosis made by the specialist.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q3
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 55-year-old man presents to his GP with a 2-week history of a painful red right eye, photophobia, and reduced vision. He wears soft contact lenses and reports wearing them overnight. On examination, the right eye shows circumcorneal injection, a hypopyon (layer of white cells in the anterior chamber), and a white corneal opacity centrally. His visual acuity in the right eye is 6/60. According to your knowledge of eye emergencies in primary care, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops and review in 48 hours',
'Prescribe oral flucloxacillin and topical lubricating eye drops and review in 1 week',
'Refer immediately to the emergency eye unit — this presentation is consistent with bacterial keratitis and hypopyon, which is a sight-threatening emergency requiring urgent specialist assessment and intensive topical antibiotic therapy',
'Reassure the patient and advise him to stop wearing contact lenses; prescribe antihistamine eye drops',
'Arrange urgent corneal swabs in primary care before referring to ophthalmology within 1 week',
'C',
'CORRECT. This presentation — painful red eye, photophobia, reduced VA, hypopyon, and central corneal opacity in a contact lens wearer — is classic for bacterial keratitis (corneal ulcer), a sight-threatening emergency. Contact lens wear (particularly overnight wear) dramatically increases the risk of keratitis, especially from Pseudomonas aeruginosa and Acanthamoeba. A hypopyon (visible layer of white cells in the anterior chamber) indicates severe anterior segment inflammation from toxins/direct invasion. Management requires same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment for: corneal scrapes for microscopy and culture, intensive topical antibiotic therapy (fluoroquinolone — ciprofloxacin or ofloxacin eye drops — every 15–30 minutes initially), and close monitoring. Delayed treatment risks corneal perforation and permanent visual impairment. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical chloramphenicol alone is insufficient for bacterial keratitis with hypopyon. Chloramphenicol does not cover Pseudomonas aeruginosa adequately — a common and highly destructive pathogen in contact lens-related keratitis. This presentation requires emergency ophthalmology assessment and intensive fluoroquinolone therapy, not primary care management.',
'Incorrect. Oral flucloxacillin treats Staphylococcal skin infections and has no role in bacterial keratitis. The intraocular penetration of oral flucloxacillin is inadequate for corneal infection. This patient has a sight-threatening emergency requiring same-day specialist assessment.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. This presentation is not allergic conjunctivitis — antihistamine drops are used for allergic eye disease (itch, bilateral symptoms, no corneal opacity). The hypopyon and corneal opacity indicate a serious infective emergency. Reassurance and antihistamines would cause dangerous delay.',
'Incorrect. Corneal swabs in primary care are not the priority — same-day emergency ophthalmology referral is. The specialist team will perform corneal scrapes in appropriate conditions with proper equipment. Attempting swabs in primary care and waiting up to a week before referral would risk permanent visual loss.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q4
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 45-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 1-week history of a painless, slowly growing lump on her right upper eyelid. The lump is approximately 5 mm in diameter, firm, non-tender, and located within the substance of the eyelid (not on the lid margin). She has no visual symptoms and her vision is normal. She has had a similar lesion before that resolved spontaneously. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and the most appropriate management?',
'Stye (hordeolum externum); prescribe topical chloramphenicol and hot compresses',
'Meibomian cyst (chalazion); advise warm compresses and lid massage; refer to ophthalmology if it fails to resolve after 4–6 weeks for consideration of incision and curettage',
'Basal cell carcinoma of the eyelid; refer urgently via 2WW suspected skin cancer pathway',
'Sebaceous cyst of the eyelid; refer to plastic surgery for excision',
'Conjunctival cyst; prescribe topical dexamethasone eye drops',
'B',
'CORRECT. A firm, non-tender, slowly growing swelling within the substance of the eyelid (not on the lid margin), with a history of a similar lesion resolving spontaneously, is classic for a meibomian cyst (chalazion). Chalazia develop from blockage of meibomian glands (sebaceous glands in the tarsal plate) causing a granulomatous lipogranuloma. Management: warm compresses (4× daily for 5–10 minutes) and lid massage to encourage drainage are first-line — the majority of chalazia resolve spontaneously within weeks to months. If it persists beyond 4–6 weeks or causes visual disturbance (from astigmatism from lid pressure), refer to ophthalmology for incision and curettage (I&C) under local anaesthesia. Topical antibiotics are not effective for chalazia as they are not infective. NICE CKS Chalazion',
'Incorrect. A stye (hordeolum externum) is an acute, painful, tender bacterial infection of a lash follicle (Zeis or Moll glands) at the lid margin — it presents as a painful red swelling at the lid margin, not within the substance of the eyelid. This patient has a painless lesion within the eyelid (tarsal plate) — consistent with a chalazion, not a stye. Chloramphenicol is appropriate for acute bacterial infections but not for chalazia.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) of the eyelid is an important diagnosis to consider for persistent eyelid lesions, particularly in older patients. BCC typically presents as a pearly nodule with rolled edges, telangiectasia, or central ulceration (rodent ulcer) — it is usually slow-growing over months to years and may cause loss of eyelashes at the lid margin. A 45-year-old with a 1-week history of a lesion similar to one she has had before is far more consistent with chalazion. However, if a chalazion fails to resolve with treatment, pathological examination of the curetted material is important to exclude malignancy.',
'Incorrect. A sebaceous cyst (epidermal inclusion cyst) of the eyelid presents as a superficial, mobile, well-defined swelling arising from the skin surface — not within the substance of the eyelid (tarsal plate). The deep location within the eyelid and the history of similar self-resolving episodes are characteristic of chalazion, not sebaceous cyst.',
'Incorrect. A conjunctival cyst presents as a transparent or semi-transparent fluid-filled swelling visible on the conjunctiva — it would not present as a firm lump within the eyelid substance. Topical dexamethasone is used for inflammatory eye conditions, not for cysts.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q5
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 70-year-old man presents to his GP with a gradual 6-month history of difficulty reading and driving at night, with glare from oncoming headlights. He describes his vision as "misty" and states colours appear washed out. His visual acuity is 6/18 in the right eye and 6/24 in the left eye. Examination of the red reflex reveals a dull, hazy appearance bilaterally. He has no pain, no flashes, and no floaters. His ocular pressure was checked recently at an optician and was normal. According to NICE guidance, what is the most appropriate management?',
'Refer urgently to the emergency eye unit as reduced red reflex indicates retinal detachment',
'Refer routinely to ophthalmology for cataract assessment; advise the patient that surgery (phacoemulsification) is considered when visual impairment significantly affects daily activities',
'Prescribe antiglare glasses and discharge as cataracts are a normal ageing process not requiring medical referral',
'Refer urgently via 2WW suspected eye cancer pathway as reduced red reflex may indicate intraocular melanoma',
'Refer to optometry for glasses prescription change as this is likely uncorrected refractive error',
'B',
'CORRECT. The clinical presentation — bilateral gradual painless vision loss with glare, washed-out colours, reduced visual acuity, and dull/hazy red reflex — is classic for bilateral cataracts (lens opacification). NICE guidance on cataracts recommends: routine referral to ophthalmology for cataract assessment when cataracts are affecting the patient''s ability to carry out daily activities (driving, reading, work). Cataract surgery (phacoemulsification with intraocular lens implantation) is the only effective treatment. NICE does not set a specific VA threshold for surgery — the decision is based on functional impact and patient preference. NHS cataract surgery is available when the cataract significantly affects quality of life. The GP should explain the referral process, set realistic expectations, and advise the patient about DVLA requirements (visual standard for driving: 6/12 Snellen on the number plate test — this patient with 6/24 left may not meet the standard). NICE',
'Incorrect. A dull, hazy red reflex (reduced but present) is characteristic of cataract — not retinal detachment. Retinal detachment presents with sudden visual loss, flashes, floaters, and a field defect. In retinal detachment, the red reflex may be absent or show a grey shadow. Emergency referral is not appropriate for bilateral cataracts.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Antiglare glasses may provide marginal relief from glare symptoms but do not treat the underlying cataract and cannot restore visual acuity to normal. Discharge without ophthalmology referral is inappropriate — this patient has significant visual impairment (6/24 in the worse eye) affecting driving safety. Referral for cataract assessment is required.',
'Incorrect. Intraocular melanoma (uveal melanoma) can occasionally reduce the red reflex if very large, but it typically presents as a unilateral, rapidly progressive condition with other features (field defect, flashes). Bilateral dull red reflex in a 70-year-old with gradual vision loss and glare symptoms is overwhelmingly consistent with cataracts. 2WW cancer referral is not appropriate here.',
'Incorrect. While refractive error can cause blurred vision, it would not produce a hazy red reflex or explain the glare and washed-out colours. Glasses cannot correct lens opacity — only cataract surgery restores vision in cataract. An optometry referral for glasses alone would not address the underlying pathology.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q6
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 65-year-old woman is reviewed at her GP surgery following a routine optician visit. The optician''s letter states: "Bilateral optic disc cupping (cup:disc ratio 0.7 bilaterally), bilateral arcuate visual field defects, IOP 26 mmHg OD and 24 mmHg OS. Strongly suspect chronic open-angle glaucoma. Please refer." She has no visual symptoms and her visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. She has a first-degree relative (mother) with glaucoma. According to NICE guidance on glaucoma, which of the following is the most appropriate action?',
'Reassure the patient and repeat optician review in 12 months as she has no visual symptoms',
'Prescribe topical timolol 0.5% eye drops immediately as IOP >21 mmHg confirms glaucoma requiring treatment',
'Refer urgently to the emergency eye unit as IOP of 26 mmHg requires same-day pressure-lowering treatment',
'Refer routinely to the hospital eye service (HES) for full glaucoma assessment including gonioscopy, visual fields, and OCT of the optic nerve; explain to the patient that she cannot drive until glaucoma is excluded',
'Refer to optometry for repeat visual field testing before hospital referral',
'D',
'CORRECT. The optician''s findings are highly suggestive of chronic open-angle glaucoma (COAG): raised IOP, increased cup:disc ratio (normal <0.5, cupping >0.6 is suspicious), and arcuate visual field defects (characteristic of glaucomatous optic nerve damage affecting the arcuate nerve fibre layer). NICE guidance on glaucoma recommends referral to the hospital eye service for formal glaucoma assessment — this should be a routine referral (not emergency, as this is a chronic condition). The HES will perform: gonioscopy, automated visual field testing, OCT of the optic nerve fibre layer, pachymetry (corneal thickness), and diurnal IOP monitoring. Treatment (prostaglandin analogue eye drops as first-line) is initiated by the specialist. Regarding driving: DVLA requires that drivers with glaucoma meet the visual field standard — the GP should advise the patient to inform the DVLA and not drive until her visual fields are formally assessed. Family history (first-degree relative) increases her risk — siblings and children of patients with glaucoma should be offered NHS-funded eye tests. NICE',
'Incorrect. Absence of visual symptoms does not reassure against glaucoma — COAG is typically asymptomatic until advanced field loss occurs. The optician has identified multiple objective signs of glaucomatous damage. A 12-month delay without hospital assessment is inappropriate and risks progressive irreversible optic nerve damage.',
'Incorrect. The GP should not initiate topical glaucoma treatment. Diagnosis of glaucoma requires formal HES assessment — IOP >21 mmHg alone does not confirm glaucoma (ocular hypertension is common and most patients do not develop glaucoma). Treatment is initiated by the specialist after full assessment.',
'Incorrect. An IOP of 26 mmHg in a patient with chronic, asymptomatic glaucoma does not require emergency same-day treatment. Emergency eye referral is for acute angle-closure glaucoma (sudden severe pain, red eye, halos, nausea, IOP typically >50 mmHg). This patient requires routine referral to HES, not emergency assessment.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. The optician has already performed visual field testing (arcuate defects identified). Repeating this in primary care or optometry before hospital referral delays appropriate specialist assessment. The GP''s role is to refer to HES — further testing should be done within the specialist service.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q7
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 78-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 3-month history of distortion of straight lines — she notices that the edges of doors and window frames appear wavy or bent. She has noticed a grey patch in her central vision in the right eye when covering the left. Her visual acuity is 6/18 in the right eye and 6/9 in the left. She is a non-smoker. Her GP performs an Amsler grid test which shows wavy lines and a central scotoma in the right eye. According to NICE guidance on age-related macular degeneration, which of the following is the most appropriate action?',
'Reassure her that central vision distortion is a normal part of ageing; prescribe vitamin C supplements',
'Refer urgently (within 1 week) to the hospital eye service for assessment of suspected wet age-related macular degeneration',
'Arrange optical coherence tomography (OCT) in primary care before referring to ophthalmology',
'Prescribe high-dose lutein and zeaxanthin supplements and review in 3 months',
'Refer routinely to ophthalmology within 6–8 weeks for macular assessment',
'B',
'CORRECT. The clinical presentation — metamorphopsia (distortion of straight lines, which appear wavy), central scotoma, and reduced VA in one eye, with a positive Amsler grid test — is classic for wet age-related macular degeneration (neovascular AMD). Wet AMD involves choroidal neovascularisation causing rapid, progressive central vision loss. NICE guidance on AMD mandates urgent referral (within 1 week) to the hospital eye service when wet AMD is suspected. This is time-critical because intravitreal anti-VEGF injections (ranibizumab, aflibercept, or bevacizumab) must be initiated promptly — delays in treatment lead to irreversible central vision loss. The GP should not delay referral for any additional primary care investigations. Dry AMD (geographic atrophy) causes gradual vision loss without metamorphopsia and does not require urgent referral. NICE',
'Incorrect. Central vision distortion (metamorphopsia) is NOT a normal part of ageing — it is a red flag symptom requiring urgent ophthalmological assessment. Vitamin C supplements have no proven role in treating established AMD. Reassurance and supplements would cause dangerous delay in a patient who requires urgent anti-VEGF treatment.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. OCT is an invaluable diagnostic tool for AMD — but it is performed by the hospital eye service, not in primary care. Delaying referral to first arrange primary care OCT (which is not routinely available in GP settings) would introduce an unacceptable delay in a condition where same-week treatment is required.',
'Incorrect. Lutein and zeaxanthin supplements (AREDS2 formulation) have evidence for slowing progression of intermediate-to-advanced dry AMD — they have no role in the acute management of wet AMD with active neovascularisation. A 3-month delay before reviewing a patient with suspected wet AMD would likely result in permanent and substantial central vision loss.',
'Incorrect. A routine 6–8 week referral is entirely inappropriate for suspected wet AMD. NICE guidance specifies urgent referral within 1 week (ideally 48–72 hours in some guidelines) for suspected neovascular AMD. A 6–8 week wait would result in irreversible visual loss from untreated choroidal neovascularisation.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q8
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 35-year-old woman who is 28 weeks pregnant presents to her GP with a 2-week history of headache, swelling of her face and hands, and blurred vision. On examination, her blood pressure is 168/108 mmHg and she has 2+ protein on urine dipstick. On ophthalmoscopy, the GP notes papilloedema bilaterally and flame haemorrhages. According to your knowledge of hypertensive eye disease and pre-eclampsia, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe labetalol 200 mg OD and arrange ophthalmology review within 2 weeks',
'Reassure her that visual changes in pregnancy are common and arrange routine antenatal review',
'Call 999 for immediate emergency transfer to the obstetric unit — this patient has severe pre-eclampsia with hypertensive retinopathy including papilloedema, representing a hypertensive emergency with risk of eclampsia and visual loss',
'Prescribe nifedipine and arrange urgent ophthalmology assessment within 48 hours',
'Arrange MRI brain urgently to exclude posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome before obstetric referral',
'C',
'CORRECT. This patient has severe pre-eclampsia (BP ≥160/110 with proteinuria and symptoms) with hypertensive retinopathy including papilloedema — a hypertensive emergency in pregnancy. Papilloedema indicates raised intracranial pressure or severe hypertensive end-organ damage. Additional visual symptoms (blurred vision) are a feature of severe pre-eclampsia and raise concern for imminent eclampsia or cortical blindness (PRES — posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome). This is a 999 emergency. Obstetric management includes: IV antihypertensive therapy (IV labetalol or IV hydralazine), IV magnesium sulphate (seizure prophylaxis), fetal monitoring, and consideration of delivery. Oral antihypertensives alone without emergency admission are insufficient. The ophthalmological findings confirm severe end-organ involvement. NICE',
'Incorrect. Oral labetalol and a 2-week ophthalmology review is wholly inadequate for a patient with BP 168/108, papilloedema, flame haemorrhages, proteinuria, and visual symptoms at 28 weeks pregnant. This is an obstetric emergency requiring immediate hospital transfer. Delay risks eclampsia, intracranial haemorrhage, placental abruption, and fetal compromise.',
'Incorrect. Visual changes in pregnancy are NOT routinely reassuring — they are a recognised warning sign of severe pre-eclampsia and must be investigated. Combined with severe hypertension, proteinuria, and papilloedema, this represents a life-threatening obstetric emergency requiring immediate 999 activation.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While PRES is a recognised complication of severe hypertension in pregnancy, investigating for it with MRI before making the obstetric emergency transfer would cause a dangerous delay. The immediate priority is emergency obstetric admission for blood pressure control and seizure prevention — MRI can be arranged by the hospital team if clinically indicated.',
'Incorrect. Nifedipine (oral) may be used for hypertension in pregnancy but is insufficient for a hypertensive emergency with papilloedema and visual symptoms at 28 weeks. This patient requires immediate emergency hospital admission with IV antihypertensives and obstetric monitoring — not an urgent ophthalmology review in 48 hours.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q9
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 60-year-old man with a 30-year history of type 1 diabetes mellitus (HbA1c 82 mmol/mol) presents to his GP. His annual diabetic eye screening report states: "R3S — proliferative diabetic retinopathy with new vessels on disc and pre-retinal haemorrhage. M1 — clinically significant macular oedema." He has no visual symptoms currently. His blood pressure is 144/88 mmHg. According to NICE guidance on diabetic retinopathy, what is the most appropriate management from primary care?',
'Refer urgently to the hospital eye service within 2 weeks for treatment; optimise glycaemic and blood pressure control as a priority',
'Refer routinely to ophthalmology within 6 weeks as he has no visual symptoms',
'Start intravitreal bevacizumab immediately in primary care to prevent progression',
'Reassure him and arrange repeat diabetic eye screening in 12 months with better glycaemic control',
'Refer urgently to the emergency eye unit today for same-day laser treatment',
'A',
'CORRECT. R3 proliferative diabetic retinopathy (PDR) with new vessels and pre-retinal haemorrhage combined with M1 clinically significant macular oedema (CSMO) represents the highest-risk diabetic eye disease findings requiring urgent ophthalmology review. NICE and the NHS Diabetic Eye Screening Programme specify: R3S (proliferative DR — new vessels) requires urgent referral to hospital eye service within 2 weeks. M1 (maculopathy) also requires fast-track referral. Treatment options include: panretinal photocoagulation (PRP) for PDR, and intravitreal anti-VEGF injections for CSMO. Crucially, the absence of visual symptoms does not mean the condition is not sight-threatening — PDR can progress rapidly to vitreous haemorrhage and tractional retinal detachment causing severe visual loss. Simultaneously, the GP must optimise the HbA1c (82 mmol/mol — significantly above target) and blood pressure as these drive retinopathy progression. NICE',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Routine 6-week referral is not appropriate for R3 proliferative diabetic retinopathy. The NHS Diabetic Eye Screening Programme classifies R3S as requiring urgent referral within 2 weeks. Lack of visual symptoms is common in early PDR but does not reduce the urgency of referral — pre-retinal haemorrhage and new vessels require prompt specialist treatment.',
'Incorrect. Intravitreal injections are a hospital procedure requiring specialist assessment, consent, and sterile conditions. GPs do not administer intravitreal therapy. The appropriate action is urgent specialist referral.',
'Incorrect. Repeat annual screening is wholly inadequate for R3 proliferative diabetic retinopathy. This is a sight-threatening condition requiring urgent specialist referral within 2 weeks — not a 12-month delay for glycaemic optimisation alone.',
'Incorrect. While PDR is serious, the same-day emergency eye unit is appropriate for acute presentations (sudden visual loss, vitreous haemorrhage, tractional retinal detachment). R3 with pre-retinal haemorrhage but no acute visual loss requires urgent referral within 2 weeks — not same-day emergency attendance, which may not be equipped for planned laser or injection treatment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q10
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 25-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 3-day history of a sore, gritty, red left eye with a mucopurulent discharge. She wakes each morning with her eyelids stuck together. She is a contact lens wearer. She has no photophobia and her visual acuity is normal. She has no history of STIs. Her right eye is unaffected. According to NICE guidance on red eye in primary care, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Refer urgently to the emergency eye unit as contact lens wearers with red eye always require same-day specialist assessment',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol 0.5% four times daily for 5 days; advise her to stop contact lens wear until fully recovered and for 48 hours after completing treatment; safety-net for worsening symptoms or photophobia',
'Prescribe topical sodium cromoglicate and advise this is likely allergic conjunctivitis',
'Arrange chlamydia swab from the eye before prescribing any treatment',
'Prescribe oral doxycycline 100 mg BD for 7 days as systemic antibiotics are required for contact lens-related conjunctivitis',
'B',
'CORRECT. This presentation — unilateral mucopurulent conjunctivitis with sticky eyelids in the morning, gritty sensation, no photophobia, normal VA — is consistent with bacterial conjunctivitis. NICE CKS on conjunctivitis recommends topical chloramphenicol 0.5% as first-line treatment for bacterial conjunctivitis (four times daily for 5–7 days or 2 days after resolution). Key safety advice for contact lens wearers: stop wearing lenses immediately during the infection, discard the current lenses (soft disposable) or thoroughly clean hard lenses, and do not resume lens wear for 48 hours after completing treatment. Safety-netting is important: if vision deteriorates, photophobia develops, or symptoms worsen despite treatment, the patient should be reassessed urgently as bacterial keratitis must be excluded. NICE',
'Incorrect. Not all red eyes in contact lens wearers require emergency referral. Bacterial conjunctivitis (mucopurulent discharge, sticky lids, normal VA, no photophobia) in a contact lens wearer can be managed in primary care with topical chloramphenicol and advice to cease lens wear. Emergency referral is required if there is photophobia, corneal opacity, pain, or reduced visual acuity — features of keratitis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Allergic conjunctivitis typically presents with bilateral itching, watery discharge, and eye rubbing — often associated with seasonal allergens or atopy. This patient has unilateral mucopurulent discharge and matted eyelids in the morning — features of bacterial, not allergic, conjunctivitis. Sodium cromoglicate would be appropriate for allergic conjunctivitis but not this presentation.',
'Incorrect. Chlamydial conjunctivitis (inclusion conjunctivitis) causes a chronic follicular conjunctivitis, usually in young sexually active adults, often bilateral, with a less acute onset. While chlamydia should be considered in the differential for chronic or recurrent conjunctivitis, this acute presentation with purulent discharge is more consistent with bacterial conjunctivitis. A chlamydia swab is not required before starting treatment in a straightforward acute case.',
'Incorrect. Systemic oral antibiotics are not indicated for straightforward bacterial conjunctivitis. Oral doxycycline is used for chlamydial conjunctivitis (which requires systemic treatment as topical antibiotics do not eradicate genital chlamydia) — but this has not been established as the diagnosis here. Topical chloramphenicol is effective and appropriate as first-line.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q11
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 52-year-old man is reviewed by his GP 6 months after starting hydroxychloroquine 400 mg OD for rheumatoid arthritis. He has no visual symptoms. His rheumatologist initiated the medication without arranging baseline ophthalmological assessment. The GP is reviewing the drug monitoring requirements. His weight is 74 kg (ideal body weight). According to NICE and Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance on hydroxychloroquine screening, which of the following most accurately describes the monitoring requirements?',
'No ophthalmological monitoring is required as hydroxychloroquine retinopathy is extremely rare at standard doses',
'Annual visual acuity checks in primary care are sufficient to detect hydroxychloroquine retinopathy',
'Hydroxychloroquine retinopathy screening requires: baseline ophthalmology assessment before or within 6 months of starting; annual screening from year 5 of treatment using optical coherence tomography and automated visual field testing',
'Hydroxychloroquine should be stopped immediately as 6 months have elapsed without a baseline ophthalmology assessment',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology as any delay in baseline assessment constitutes a prescribing error requiring immediate specialist review',
'C',
'CORRECT. Hydroxychloroquine can cause irreversible toxic maculopathy (bull''s eye maculopathy) — the risk is dose-dependent and increases with duration of use (particularly after 5 years). The Royal College of Ophthalmologists and NICE guidance specify: (1) Baseline assessment: within 6 months of starting hydroxychloroquine — to document pre-existing eye conditions and establish a baseline; (2) Annual screening from year 5 onwards: using OCT (to detect parafoveal thinning) and automated visual field testing (Humphrey visual field testing with central 10-2 protocol). Screening before year 5 is not routinely indicated as retinopathy risk is very low in the first 5 years at standard doses; (3) Safe dosing: the daily dose should not exceed 5 mg/kg actual body weight (not ideal body weight); this patient is on 400 mg OD with actual weight 74 kg = 5.4 mg/kg — slightly above the threshold, which should be flagged to the prescribing rheumatologist. NICE',
'Incorrect. While hydroxychloroquine retinopathy is uncommon, it is not rare and is irreversible once established. The Royal College of Ophthalmologists and NICE guidance specify structured screening requirements — dismissing the need for monitoring could lead to avoidable permanent visual loss.',
'Incorrect. Annual visual acuity checks in primary care are NOT adequate for hydroxychloroquine screening. Visual acuity is often preserved until late in the disease. The recommended screening requires OCT (sensitive for early parafoveal thinning before symptoms develop) and automated visual field testing — both requiring specialist equipment.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Stopping hydroxychloroquine at 6 months because baseline assessment has not yet been done would deprive a patient of an effective treatment for rheumatoid arthritis. The correct action is to arrange the baseline assessment now (it is still within an acceptable timeframe) and continue the medication. Abrupt cessation of hydroxychloroquine risks disease flare.',
'Incorrect. A 6-month delay in baseline assessment, while not ideal, does not constitute a prescribing error requiring urgent specialist review as an emergency. The appropriate action is to arrange the baseline ophthalmology assessment promptly and ensure the annual screening protocol is established from year 5. Urgent specialist review is not required for an asymptomatic patient.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q12
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 40-year-old man presents to his GP with a 2-day history of a painless, bright red area covering the temporal aspect of the right conjunctiva. He has no discharge, no photophobia, and his vision is normal. He reports he was straining heavily at the gym the previous day. He is on aspirin 75 mg OD for a previous TIA. On examination, the area is flat, well-demarcated, and bright red against a white background. The rest of the eye is white. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and appropriate management?',
'Bacterial conjunctivitis; prescribe topical chloramphenicol',
'Episcleritis; prescribe topical NSAIDs and review in 1 week',
'Subconjunctival haemorrhage; reassure the patient that this resolves spontaneously within 2–3 weeks; no treatment required; check blood pressure and review anticoagulation if recurrent',
'Acute angle-closure glaucoma; refer immediately to the emergency eye unit',
'Scleritis; refer urgently to ophthalmology within 48 hours',
'C',
'CORRECT. A subconjunctival haemorrhage (SCH) is caused by rupture of small conjunctival blood vessels, producing a flat, well-demarcated, bright red patch against a white background — with no discharge, no pain, and no visual disturbance. Precipitating factors include: Valsalva manoeuvre (straining, coughing, vomiting, heavy lifting — as in this case), hypertension, anticoagulant/antiplatelet medication, trauma, or bleeding disorders. Management: reassure the patient — SCH resolves spontaneously within 2–3 weeks as the blood is reabsorbed. No treatment is required. However, the GP should: check blood pressure (hypertension is a common cause), note the aspirin use (antiplatelet drugs increase bleeding tendency), and if recurrences are frequent, consider checking FBC, coagulation, and blood pressure more comprehensively. NICE CKS Subconjunctival haemorrhage',
'Incorrect. Bacterial conjunctivitis presents with bilateral or unilateral sticky discharge, mucopurulent exudate, and diffuse conjunctival injection (pinkish-red appearance throughout the conjunctiva) — not a well-demarcated flat bright red patch against an otherwise white eye. Chloramphenicol is not appropriate for subconjunctival haemorrhage.',
'Incorrect. Episcleritis presents as a localised area of redness (nodular or diffuse) in the episcleral tissue, which is ELEVATED above the conjunctiva, and may be associated with mild discomfort or tenderness. The eye has a salmon-pink or red nodular appearance. Subconjunctival haemorrhage is flat, bright red, and clearly demarcated — a different clinical appearance.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Acute angle-closure glaucoma presents with: sudden severe eye pain, headache, nausea, halos around lights, red eye, and a rock-hard eye on palpation with a mid-dilated fixed pupil. This patient has no pain, no haloes, no reduced vision, and a normal-feeling eye — there is nothing consistent with acute angle-closure glaucoma.',
'Incorrect. Scleritis presents with a deep, boring, severe ocular pain (often waking the patient at night), tenderness to touch, and a violaceous (purple-red) discolouration of the sclera. It is associated with systemic autoimmune diseases (RA, SLE, vasculitis). This patient has no pain and a bright red flat patch — consistent with subconjunctival haemorrhage, not scleritis.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q13
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 28-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 5-day history of bilateral red, itchy, watery eyes. She also has a blocked and runny nose, sneezing, and nasal itching. She is a known hay fever sufferer and her symptoms typically worsen in spring and summer. She has no discharge, no pain, and normal visual acuity. She has previously used antihistamine tablets which helped partially. On examination, both eyes are mildly injected with no corneal involvement. According to NICE guidance on allergic conjunctivitis, which of the following represents the most appropriate treatment step-up from antihistamine tablets?',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol as she likely has concurrent bacterial conjunctivitis',
'Prescribe topical sodium cromoglicate or nedocromil eye drops alongside a non-sedating oral antihistamine for combined allergic rhinoconjunctivitis',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology as bilateral red eyes always require specialist exclusion of keratitis',
'Prescribe topical corticosteroid eye drops as first-line for allergic conjunctivitis',
'Prescribe topical oxymetazoline as a decongestant eye drop for immediate symptom relief',
'B',
'CORRECT. Allergic conjunctivitis associated with allergic rhinitis (hay fever) is extremely common. Management follows a step-up approach per NICE CKS: (1) Non-pharmacological: cold compresses, avoidance of allergens, artificial tears for dilution; (2) Mild symptoms: non-sedating oral antihistamine (cetirizine, loratadine) — already tried; (3) Moderate/persistent symptoms: add topical mast cell stabiliser (sodium cromoglicate 2% or nedocromil 2% eye drops) — these prevent mast cell degranulation and reduce histamine release; alternatively, topical antihistamine eye drops (olopatadine, ketotifen) which have both antihistamine and mast cell stabilising properties; (4) Intranasal corticosteroid spray (for nasal symptoms); (5) Topical ocular corticosteroids are not recommended routinely by NICE for allergic conjunctivitis due to risk of serious side effects (cataract, glaucoma, infection) — specialist supervision required. NICE',
'Incorrect. Bacterial conjunctivitis produces mucopurulent discharge and matted eyelids — not bilateral itchy watery eyes with nasal allergy symptoms. This presentation is classic allergic conjunctivitis. Topical chloramphenicol has no role in allergic conjunctivitis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Bilateral red itchy watery eyes with a clear seasonal allergic rhinoconjunctivitis history and no photophobia, pain, corneal involvement, or reduced vision does not require urgent ophthalmology referral. This is straightforward allergic conjunctivitis appropriately managed in primary care.',
'Incorrect. Topical corticosteroid eye drops (dexamethasone, prednisolone) are not recommended as first-line for allergic conjunctivitis by NICE due to significant risks of complications: posterior subcapsular cataract, raised intraocular pressure, and susceptibility to corneal infections (herpes simplex keratitis). They are used under specialist supervision for severe vernal keratoconjunctivitis, not for routine seasonal allergic conjunctivitis.',
'Incorrect. Topical decongestant eye drops (alpha-adrenergic agonists such as oxymetazoline or naphazoline) cause vasoconstriction and temporary whitening of the eye but do not treat the underlying allergic mechanism. They cause rebound redness with prolonged use and are not recommended by NICE for allergic conjunctivitis management.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q14
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 65-year-old man presents to his GP with a painful right eye for 3 days. He has a vesicular rash on the right side of his forehead and scalp, extending to the tip of his nose. His right eye is red and watery. Visual acuity is 6/9 in the right eye. On examination, there are pseudodendritic corneal lesions visible with fluorescein staining. He has never had shingles before. According to your knowledge of herpes zoster ophthalmicus, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical aciclovir cream to the skin rash only and review in 1 week',
'Start oral aciclovir 800 mg five times daily for 7–10 days immediately and refer urgently to ophthalmology the same day',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol eye drops for the conjunctivitis and oral prednisolone for the rash',
'Reassure that herpes zoster ophthalmicus is self-limiting and prescribe paracetamol for pain',
'Refer to dermatology for management of the shingles rash before addressing the eye symptoms',
'B',
'CORRECT. Herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO) occurs when varicella-zoster virus reactivates in the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve (V1). Key diagnostic features: (1) Hutchinson''s sign — vesicular rash on the tip or side of the nose (nasociliary branch of V1) strongly predicts ocular involvement — present here; (2) Pseudodendrites on fluorescein staining (compared to true dendrites of HSV keratitis — VZV dendrites are smaller and have tapered ends without terminal bulbs). Complications include: keratitis, uveitis, episcleritis, scleritis, optic neuritis, retinal necrosis, and post-herpetic neuralgia. Management: (1) Oral antiviral (aciclovir 800 mg 5× daily or valaciclovir 1 g TDS) for 7–10 days — must be started within 72 hours of rash onset for maximum benefit; (2) Same-day emergency ophthalmology referral as corneal involvement (pseudodendrites) has been confirmed — this requires topical antiviral drops and specialist management; (3) Analgesia; (4) Consider gabapentin for neuropathic pain prevention. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical aciclovir cream is for labial herpes (cold sores) — it is not absorbed systemically and does not treat the virus reactivating in the nerve ganglion. Oral antivirals are required for HZO. A 1-week review without ophthalmic assessment is dangerous when corneal involvement has been identified.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Topical chloramphenicol treats bacterial conjunctivitis — it has no antiviral activity and will not treat VZV keratitis. Oral prednisolone alone without antiviral cover in HZO could worsen viral replication. This management plan is inappropriate and potentially harmful.',
'Incorrect. HZO with corneal involvement (pseudodendrites on fluorescein staining) is NOT self-limiting — without antiviral treatment and specialist eye care, it can cause corneal scarring, anterior uveitis, raised IOP, optic neuritis, and permanent visual loss. Reassurance alone is entirely inappropriate.',
'Incorrect. The eye manifestations of HZO are the priority — dermatology input for the skin rash is not the immediate management. The eye needs same-day ophthalmology assessment and systemic antiviral treatment must be started immediately. The skin rash of shingles does not require dermatology referral — it is managed by the GP with oral antivirals.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q15
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 50-year-old man presents to his GP requesting driving advice. He has been told by his optician that he has a visual field defect in his right eye from previous optic neuritis. He drives a private car (Group 1 licence). His optician has performed an Esterman binocular visual field test which shows he fails to meet the DVLA standard. He currently holds a full driving licence and denies telling the DVLA. According to current DVLA medical standards for driving, which of the following is the most appropriate action for the GP?',
'Advise the patient to inform the DVLA himself and document the advice given; if he refuses or fails to notify, the GP has a duty to inform the DVLA directly after warning the patient',
'The GP has no legal obligation to report driving fitness — this is entirely the patient''s responsibility',
'Immediately contact the DVLA without informing the patient to protect road safety',
'Advise the patient to stop driving immediately and surrender his licence without notifying the DVLA as this would breach patient confidentiality',
'The optician''s Esterman field test is not accepted by the DVLA — arrange a formal DVLA-approved field test before any advice is given',
'A',
'CORRECT. The DVLA visual field standard for Group 1 (car/motorcycle) licence holders requires: a minimum field of vision of at least 120 degrees on the horizontal meridian, with no significant defect within the central 20 degrees. An Esterman binocular visual field test is the DVLA-approved test for assessing fitness to drive. If a patient fails this standard, they must not drive and must inform the DVLA. The GP''s role and obligations: (1) Advise the patient he must inform the DVLA and must stop driving; (2) Document this advice in the medical records; (3) If the patient refuses to notify the DVLA or continues to drive, the GP is ethically and legally justified in contacting the DVLA directly after warning the patient of this intention — this is an exception to patient confidentiality where public safety is at risk (GMC guidance on confidentiality supports disclosure to prevent serious harm). NICE/DVLA/GMC',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. The GP does have a responsibility in situations where a patient poses a risk to road safety. GMC guidance on confidentiality permits — and in cases of serious risk, supports — disclosure to the DVLA when a patient refuses to notify them. The GP''s responsibility is not purely passive.',
'Incorrect. Contacting the DVLA without first warning the patient and giving them the opportunity to self-report would breach the process recommended by GMC guidance. The correct sequence is: advise the patient, document the advice, and if they refuse to report, warn them that the GP will contact the DVLA directly — then do so if they still refuse.',
'Incorrect. Advising the patient to stop driving without notifying the DVLA is incomplete advice. The patient must both stop driving AND inform the DVLA — the licence may need to be revoked or modified. Simply surrendering the licence to the DVLA is also not the correct mechanism — the DVLA needs to be informed of the medical condition.',
'Incorrect. The Esterman binocular visual field test IS the DVLA-approved assessment for Group 1 drivers. An optician-performed Esterman test result is clinically valid and widely accepted. There is no need to arrange a separate "DVLA-approved" test — the Esterman test performed by the optician is the appropriate assessment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

-- Q16 - Q40 continuing with equally detailed questions
(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 6-week-old baby is brought to the GP by her mother who has noticed the baby''s left eye appears to have a white pupil (leukocoria) in photographs. The right eye appears normal. The baby was born at term with no perinatal complications. On examination using the direct ophthalmoscope, the right eye has a normal bright red reflex. The left eye has an absent or white reflex. There is no obvious eye movement abnormality. According to current guidance on newborn eye examination and leukocoria, which of the following is the most appropriate action?',
'Reassure the mother that leukocoria in photographs is commonly caused by camera flash and is normal',
'Refer to optometry for glasses assessment as leukocoria indicates significant refractive error',
'Refer immediately (same day) to paediatric ophthalmology — leukocoria is a red flag for retinoblastoma and other serious intraocular conditions requiring urgent specialist assessment',
'Arrange urgent MRI of the orbits before ophthalmology referral to characterise the lesion',
'Refer to the neonatal unit for re-examination as the newborn check may have been missed',
'C',
'CORRECT. Leukocoria (white pupil reflex) in an infant is a paediatric and ophthalmological emergency. The differential diagnosis includes: retinoblastoma (most serious — a malignant intraocular tumour of childhood; leukocoria is the most common presenting sign), congenital cataract, persistent fetal vasculature (PFV), retinopathy of prematurity (ROP — though this baby is term), Coats disease, and toxocariasis. Retinoblastoma is the most feared cause — it is potentially life-threatening if diagnosis is delayed. Same-day referral to a paediatric ophthalmologist is mandatory for all cases of leukocoria in infants. The absent red reflex should also have been detected at the newborn physical examination (6-week check includes examination of the red reflex). NICE neonatal screening guidelines mandate red reflex testing at birth and 6–8 weeks. NICE',
'Incorrect. Leukocoria in photographs is NOT a normal finding from camera flash — it is an abnormal white pupil reflex that requires urgent investigation. Normal camera flash produces a red reflex from the retina. A white pupil reflex (from intraocular tumour, cataract, or other pathology) produces the white appearance. Reassurance would be catastrophically wrong — retinoblastoma is a potentially fatal malignancy if not diagnosed promptly.',
'Incorrect. Leukocoria is not caused by refractive error. Refractive errors (myopia, hypermetropia) do not produce a white pupil reflex. Optometry referral is inappropriate — this requires urgent same-day paediatric ophthalmology assessment to exclude retinoblastoma.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. MRI may form part of the subsequent workup for orbital/intraocular lesions, but it should be arranged by the specialist team — not in primary care before the urgent ophthalmology referral. Delaying the referral for primary care imaging would cause an unacceptable delay in diagnosing a potentially life-threatening condition.',
'Incorrect. The neonatal unit is not the appropriate referral destination for a 6-week-old infant with leukocoria. The GP should refer directly and urgently to paediatric ophthalmology. The failure to detect the absent red reflex at the newborn check should be documented but does not change the immediate management.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 38-year-old man presents to his GP with a 3-day history of right eye pain, redness, and photophobia. He has noticed his vision is slightly blurred. He has a history of ankylosing spondylitis diagnosed 5 years ago. On examination, the right eye is injected with circumcorneal (ciliary) flush, and the pupil appears slightly smaller than the left and irregular. He has no skin rash and no recent viral illness. According to your knowledge of uveitis associated with HLA-B27 conditions, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol and advise the patient to return in 1 week',
'Refer same-day to the emergency eye unit — this presentation is consistent with acute anterior uveitis requiring urgent specialist assessment, mydriatics, and topical corticosteroids',
'Prescribe oral NSAIDs for his ankylosing spondylitis flare as the eye symptoms are likely related to systemic inflammation',
'Reassure the patient and advise over-the-counter lubricating drops as the symptoms are minor',
'Prescribe topical timolol to reduce intraocular pressure as uveitis commonly causes raised IOP',
'B',
'CORRECT. Acute anterior uveitis (AAU) — also called iritis — is the most common form of uveitis and is strongly associated with HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathies (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, IBD-associated arthritis). Classic features: unilateral painful red eye, photophobia, circumcorneal (ciliary) flush (injection around the limbus), small irregular pupil (posterior synechiae — adhesions between iris and lens), and blurred vision. This is an ophthalmic emergency requiring same-day specialist assessment. Complications of untreated uveitis: posterior synechiae leading to irregular pupil, raised IOP, cataract, cystoid macular oedema, and vision loss. Treatment by the specialist: topical corticosteroids (prednisolone acetate), cyclopentolate/atropine (cycloplegia — dilates pupil, relieves pain, prevents synechiae formation). NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical chloramphenicol treats bacterial conjunctivitis and has no role in uveitis management. Uveitis with circumcorneal flush, irregular small pupil, and photophobia requires same-day emergency eye assessment — not primary care antibiotic treatment and a 1-week review.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Oral NSAIDs may help with systemic ankylosing spondylitis symptoms but do not treat acute anterior uveitis. Uveitis requires topical and sometimes systemic corticosteroids initiated by an ophthalmologist, and cycloplegic agents to prevent posterior synechiae formation. Treating with NSAIDs alone risks permanent eye damage.',
'Incorrect. Acute anterior uveitis presents with photophobia, pain, and circumcorneal flush — these are not minor symptoms requiring only lubricating drops. Over-the-counter drops would provide no benefit and dangerous delay would occur.',
'Incorrect. Topical timolol (a beta-blocker) reduces IOP and is used for glaucoma management. While uveitis can cause secondary glaucoma, prescribing IOP-lowering medication in primary care without first confirming the diagnosis and baseline IOP via specialist assessment is not appropriate. The immediate priority is same-day emergency eye referral.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 55-year-old man presents to his GP complaining of a lump on his right lower eyelid that has been growing slowly for 4 months. He initially thought it was a stye but it has not resolved. On examination there is a firm, non-tender, pearly nodule approximately 8 mm in diameter on the lower lid margin. The lesion has irregular edges, small telangiectatic vessels, and there is loss of eyelashes (madarosis) at the lid margin adjacent to the lesion. He is a fair-skinned man with significant sun exposure history. According to NICE guidance on suspected skin cancer, what is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe a topical antibiotic and review in 4 weeks as this is likely a persistent stye',
'Refer urgently via the 2WW suspected skin cancer pathway — the features (pearly nodule, irregular edges, telangiectasia, madarosis) are highly suspicious for basal cell carcinoma of the eyelid',
'Reassure the patient and incise the lesion in primary care as a chalazion treatment',
'Prescribe topical imiquimod cream as this is first-line treatment for eyelid BCC',
'Arrange dermoscopy in primary care before deciding on urgency of referral',
'B',
'CORRECT. The clinical features are highly suspicious for basal cell carcinoma (BCC) of the eyelid — the most common periocular malignancy (accounting for approximately 90% of eyelid malignancies): pearly nodule with irregular edges, telangiectasia, and madarosis (loss of eyelashes — a critical sign indicating invasion of the lash follicles by tumour, differentiating malignancy from chalazion). BCCs most commonly affect the lower eyelid and medial canthus. NICE NG12 mandates urgent 2WW referral for any lesion suspicious for BCC, particularly in the periocular region. Periocular BCCs require specialist management — ophthalmology or oculoplastic surgery — due to the anatomical complexity and risk of orbital invasion. Primary care incision or topical imiquimod is inappropriate for periocular lesions that may require Mohs surgery. NICE',
'Incorrect. A persistent eyelid lesion with madarosis, telangiectasia, and pearly edges is NOT a stye. Styes are acute, painful, tender inflammatory lesions at the lid margin. A 4-month history of a persistent growing pearly nodule with these features requires urgent cancer pathway referral, not topical antibiotics.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Incising a suspected BCC in primary care as a chalazion would be a significant clinical error — it would disrupt the tumour margin, make subsequent histological assessment of margins more difficult, and delay appropriate oncological management. Suspected malignancy must be referred, not treated empirically in primary care.',
'Incorrect. Topical imiquimod may have a role in some superficial BCCs but is contraindicated for periocular lesions (risk of ocular irritation and inadequate treatment of nodular or infiltrative types). Moreover, treatment should only follow histological confirmation by a specialist — primary care prescribing of imiquimod for an unconfirmed eyelid lesion is inappropriate.',
'Incorrect. Dermoscopy can support clinical assessment of skin lesions but its availability in primary care is limited. More importantly, the clinical features here — madarosis, pearly nodule, telangiectasia, 4-month growth — are sufficiently alarming to mandate 2WW referral without delay for dermoscopy. When cancer is clinically suspected, the 2WW pathway should not be delayed by additional primary care assessments.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 70-year-old woman is reviewed by her GP following discharge from hospital after a right-sided cortical stroke. The discharge summary notes "right homonymous hemianopia." She is asking about returning to driving. She holds a Group 1 driving licence. According to DVLA medical standards for visual field defects, which of the following is the most appropriate advice?',
'She can return to driving immediately as homonymous hemianopia only affects peripheral vision which is not required for driving',
'She must not drive and must notify the DVLA — homonymous hemianopia fails the DVLA visual field standard for Group 1 licence holders regardless of which side is affected',
'She can drive using only mirrors to compensate for the field defect — no DVLA notification is required',
'She must wait 1 month before returning to driving and does not need to notify the DVLA as the stroke was her first',
'She can return to driving after completing a driving assessment course — DVLA notification is optional for post-stroke patients',
'B',
'CORRECT. Homonymous hemianopia (loss of vision in the same half of the visual field in both eyes) fails the DVLA visual field standard for Group 1 (car/motorcycle) drivers. The DVLA standard requires: a minimum field of vision of at least 120 degrees on the horizontal meridian, and no significant defect within the central 20 degrees. A homonymous hemianopia by definition eliminates one entire half of the visual field — this does not meet the standard. The patient MUST: stop driving immediately, notify the DVLA of her visual field defect (the DVLA will arrange a formal Esterman binocular field assessment), and not resume driving until the DVLA has assessed and approved her fitness to drive. After a stroke, the DVLA also requires a minimum of 1 month off driving for all stroke patients (Group 1). She must be advised of both the visual field requirement and the post-stroke driving restriction. NICE/DVLA',
'Incorrect. Homonymous hemianopia fails the DVLA visual field standard — it eliminates an entire half of the visual field which is far beyond the permissible defect for driving. It is not a minor peripheral defect — it significantly impairs the ability to detect pedestrians, cyclists, and other vehicles on the affected side.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Using mirrors does not compensate for homonymous hemianopia in terms of meeting the DVLA visual field standard. The Esterman binocular visual field test assesses the integrated visual field — mirror compensation is not an accepted substitute. DVLA notification is mandatory.',
'Incorrect. While the minimum 1-month post-stroke driving restriction applies (which is correct), DVLA notification IS required after a stroke — not optional. After 1 month, she would also need to meet the visual field standard which, with a homonymous hemianopia, she cannot. DVLA notification of both the stroke and the visual field defect is mandatory.',
'Incorrect. A driving assessment course (such as those offered by the Driving Mobility network) can be arranged to assess driving safety, but it does not replace DVLA notification or medical fitness to drive standards. DVLA notification is NOT optional for patients with homonymous hemianopia or post-stroke.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 45-year-old woman presents to her GP for an annual medication review. She has been taking chlorpromazine 200 mg OD for chronic schizophrenia for 12 years. She has no visual complaints. Her GP is reviewing long-term drug monitoring requirements. According to your knowledge of phenothiazine antipsychotic ocular side effects, which of the following is correct regarding ophthalmological monitoring for chlorpromazine?',
'No ophthalmological monitoring is required as ocular side effects of chlorpromazine are purely cosmetic',
'Annual slit-lamp examination is required after 10 years of cumulative dose exceeding 800g as chlorpromazine can cause corneal and lens deposits and pigmentary retinopathy',
'Annual OCT scanning is required from year 1 of treatment regardless of dose',
'Ophthalmological monitoring is only required if the patient reports visual symptoms',
'Chlorpromazine ocular deposits are only associated with doses above 1000 mg daily and are not relevant at 200 mg OD',
'B',
'CORRECT. Chlorpromazine (a phenothiazine antipsychotic) can cause dose-dependent and duration-dependent ocular side effects: (1) Corneal and lens deposits (stellate anterior lens opacities and corneal opacification) — typically fine white-yellow granular deposits; (2) Pigmentary retinopathy — rare but serious, characterised by pigmentary changes in the peripheral retina that can affect vision; these are associated with long-term high cumulative dose. The Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance and the BNF recommend ophthalmological examination (slit-lamp examination) for patients who have received chlorpromazine for extended periods, particularly when the cumulative dose exceeds approximately 800g. At 200 mg/day for 12 years, this patient has received approximately 876g cumulative dose — she should be referred for ophthalmological assessment. Thioridazine (another phenothiazine) carries a higher retinopathy risk and is now withdrawn in the UK. NICE',
'Incorrect. Chlorpromazine ocular deposits are a significant clinical concern — not merely cosmetic. Pigmentary retinopathy can cause visual impairment and is irreversible once established. Monitoring is clinically important.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Annual OCT from year 1 is not the recommended monitoring protocol for chlorpromazine — this level of monitoring is not required at standard doses in early treatment. The monitoring threshold is linked to cumulative dose (approximately 800g) and treatment duration.',
'Incorrect. Waiting for symptoms before monitoring is inadequate — pigmentary retinopathy may be asymptomatic in its early stages. Proactive monitoring allows detection before irreversible visual loss occurs.',
'Incorrect. Chlorpromazine retinopathy risk is related to cumulative total dose, not solely to the daily dose. A patient on 200 mg daily for 12 years (cumulative approximately 876g) is at significant cumulative dose risk. The 800g cumulative threshold is the relevant parameter, not the daily dose in isolation.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 14-year-old boy is brought to the GP by his mother. She is concerned because his optician has prescribed increasingly strong glasses each year for the past 3 years. His current prescription is -4.50 DS in both eyes. He spends approximately 8 hours per day on screens and rarely goes outdoors. He has no other medical history. According to current evidence and NICE guidance on myopia management in children, which of the following most accurately reflects the recommended advice for this family?',
'Myopia always stabilises at puberty — reassure the family and continue with glasses updates annually',
'Advise at least 2 hours of outdoor time per day as there is strong evidence that outdoor light exposure reduces the progression of myopia in children; discuss orthokeratology or low-dose atropine with an optometrist as myopia control interventions',
'Prescribe oral vitamin A supplementation as deficiency is the primary cause of childhood myopia progression',
'Advise the family that contact lenses are the only proven method to slow myopia progression in children',
'Reduce screen time to zero as digital device use is the only proven cause of myopia progression',
'B',
'CORRECT. Progressive childhood myopia is a growing public health concern. Evidence-based advice for myopia management in children: (1) Outdoor time: strong epidemiological and RCT evidence demonstrates that spending at least 2 hours per day outdoors (regardless of physical activity level) significantly reduces the rate of myopia onset and progression — the mechanism involves high-intensity outdoor light stimulating dopamine release in the retina, which inhibits axial elongation; (2) Myopia control interventions (discussed with optometrist): low-dose atropine 0.01% eye drops (significantly reduces axial elongation with minimal side effects), orthokeratology (OK lenses — rigid contact lenses worn overnight that temporarily reshape the cornea), and MiSight daily contact lenses are all evidence-based myopia control options; (3) Screen time reduction is sensible but the evidence that screen time directly causes myopia is weaker than the evidence for protective effects of outdoor light. NICE',
'Incorrect. Myopia does not always stabilise at puberty — it can continue to progress into early adulthood, particularly in those with high myopia. Waiting without myopia control intervention while the child develops increasingly high myopia risks complications of high myopia (retinal detachment, myopic maculopathy, glaucoma). Proactive management is appropriate.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Vitamin A deficiency causes night blindness and eventual corneal disease (xerophthalmia) — it is not a cause of myopia progression. Vitamin A supplementation has no role in myopia management in children in the UK where nutritional vitamin A deficiency is rare.',
'Incorrect. Contact lenses alone (standard soft contact lenses) do not slow myopia progression. However, specifically designed contact lenses for myopia control (MiSight, orthokeratology) do have evidence — but these are specialist interventions, not standard contact lenses. The claim that contact lenses are the ONLY proven method is also incorrect — atropine and outdoor time also have strong evidence.',
'Incorrect. While reducing screen time is sensible lifestyle advice, the evidence that screen time directly and independently causes myopia progression is not as strong as the evidence for protective outdoor time. Advising complete elimination of screens is impractical and not the key evidence-based recommendation. The emphasis should be on increasing outdoor time, not eliminating screen time completely.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 55-year-old woman presents to her GP after being told by her optician that she has "dry AMD." The optician''s letter states: "Multiple soft drusen bilaterally, no evidence of neovascularisation. Diagnosed with intermediate dry age-related macular degeneration." She has mild visual difficulty reading in dim light. According to NICE guidance on AMD and evidence-based supplementation, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology within 1 week as intermediate dry AMD always requires anti-VEGF injections',
'Recommend AREDS2 formulation supplements (lutein, zeaxanthin, vitamin C, vitamin E, zinc) as these have been shown to reduce progression to advanced AMD in intermediate AMD; advise on smoking cessation as smoking doubles AMD risk; refer routinely to ophthalmology for monitoring',
'Reassure her that dry AMD never progresses to sight-threatening disease and no treatment is available or required',
'Prescribe intravitreal ranibizumab immediately as this is the standard of care for all AMD regardless of type',
'Advise her to eat more fish and carrots and recheck at her next annual optician appointment',
'B',
'CORRECT. Dry AMD (non-neovascular AMD) is characterised by drusen (deposits under the retinal pigment epithelium) and geographic atrophy. Intermediate AMD (multiple soft drusen) carries a significant 5-year risk of progression to advanced AMD (either wet AMD or geographic atrophy causing severe vision loss). Evidence-based management: (1) AREDS2 supplements: the Age-Related Eye Disease Study 2 demonstrated that a specific antioxidant/mineral supplement formulation (lutein 10mg + zeaxanthin 2mg + vitamin C 500mg + vitamin E 400IU + zinc 80mg + copper 2mg) reduces the risk of progression from intermediate to advanced AMD by approximately 25% over 5 years — this is the only intervention proven to slow dry AMD progression; (2) Smoking cessation: smoking is the strongest modifiable risk factor for AMD and doubles the risk; (3) Routine ophthalmology referral for monitoring; (4) Patient education about symptoms of wet AMD conversion (metamorphopsia, rapid visual loss) and urgent self-referral advice. NICE',
'Incorrect. Intermediate dry AMD does not require urgent 1-week ophthalmology referral — anti-VEGF injections are used for wet (neovascular) AMD only. Dry AMD does not have a proven anti-VEGF treatment. Urgent referral is appropriate if there is suspicion of conversion to wet AMD (metamorphopsia, rapid visual deterioration).',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Dry AMD can progress to severe visual loss through geographic atrophy, and approximately 10–15% of dry AMD cases convert to wet AMD (which is more rapidly sight-threatening). Stating that dry AMD never causes sight-threatening disease is incorrect and may cause the patient to disregard important monitoring and symptoms.',
'Incorrect. Intravitreal ranibizumab (anti-VEGF) is used for wet (neovascular) AMD — it treats choroidal neovascularisation by inhibiting VEGF-driven new vessel growth. It has no proven role in dry AMD. Prescribing anti-VEGF injections for dry AMD would be inappropriate.',
'Incorrect. While a diet rich in leafy greens, fish, and colourful vegetables is associated with lower AMD risk, advising fish and carrots without recommending AREDS2 supplements misses the evidence-based intervention. The AREDS2 study established a specific supplement formulation — not general dietary advice — as the intervention that reduces progression risk. Annual optician review without specialist ophthalmology input may be inadequate for intermediate AMD.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 42-year-old man presents to the GP. His optician has written a referral letter stating: "Intraocular pressure 28 mmHg OD, 26 mmHg OS on two separate measurements. Cup:disc ratio 0.55 bilaterally. Normal visual fields. No disc haemorrhages. Diagnosed as ocular hypertension (OHT). Please refer." He has no visual symptoms. His central corneal thickness (CCT) measured by the optician is 590 microns bilaterally. His 10-year cardiovascular risk of developing glaucoma is assessed as low. According to NICE guidance on ocular hypertension and glaucoma, which of the following most accurately describes the appropriate management?',
'Start topical prostaglandin analogue eye drops immediately as IOP >21 mmHg in both eyes confirms glaucoma requiring treatment',
'Refer to the hospital eye service for formal glaucoma assessment; if OHT is confirmed with low risk of conversion to glaucoma, offer monitoring rather than immediate treatment',
'Discharge back to optometry annual review as IOP of 28 mmHg is within normal limits for some patients',
'Refer urgently to the emergency eye unit as IOP >25 mmHg bilaterally is a glaucomatous emergency',
'Prescribe oral acetazolamide 250 mg QDS immediately to lower IOP acutely',
'B',
'CORRECT. Ocular hypertension (OHT) is defined as IOP >21 mmHg with normal optic disc appearance, normal visual fields, and no glaucomatous damage. NICE guidance on glaucoma and OHT recommends: (1) Referral to the hospital eye service for formal assessment; (2) At HES, a risk model (such as the Moorfields Ocular Hypertension Risk Calculator or the NICE OHT risk model) is applied using: IOP level, CCT, age, optic disc appearance, and other risk factors to calculate 3-year and 5-year risk of converting to glaucoma; (3) Low-risk OHT: monitoring without treatment (IOP-lowering treatment does not provide net benefit in low-risk OHT per NICE); (4) High-risk OHT (>6% risk of conversion at 2 years): offer IOP-lowering treatment (usually prostaglandin analogue eye drops). CCT of 590 microns is within normal range (average CCT ~540 microns — thicker corneas give falsely high tonometry readings, so true IOP may be lower). NICE',
'Incorrect. A diagnosis of ocular hypertension does not equal glaucoma — glaucoma requires optic nerve damage and/or visual field loss. IOP >21 mmHg alone does not constitute glaucoma. Initiating treatment without HES assessment and risk stratification is premature. NICE guidance requires HES assessment before treatment decisions.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. IOP of 28 mmHg bilaterally is above the normal range (>21 mmHg) and does require referral to the hospital eye service. Discharging to optometry annual review alone is insufficient — formal specialist assessment including OCT, pachymetry, and visual field testing is required to properly risk-stratify this patient.',
'Incorrect. IOP of 26–28 mmHg in asymptomatic OHT is not an emergency. The emergency eye unit is appropriate for acute angle-closure glaucoma (IOP typically >50 mmHg with symptoms of pain, haloes, vomiting). Stable asymptomatic OHT requires routine HES referral, not emergency assessment.',
'Incorrect. Oral acetazolamide is used for acute IOP lowering in angle-closure glaucoma — not for stable asymptomatic OHT. Its side effect profile (metabolic acidosis, renal calculi, electrolyte disturbance, paraesthesia) makes it inappropriate for long-term outpatient use. Topical prostaglandin analogues are used if IOP-lowering treatment is indicated, not oral acetazolamide.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 67-year-old man presents to his GP for review after a transient ischaemic attack (TIA) 3 weeks ago, which presented as sudden painless monocular vision loss in his right eye lasting 5 minutes. This is called amaurosis fugax. He is currently on aspirin 300 mg OD, which was started in the emergency department. He smokes 15 cigarettes daily and has a total cholesterol of 5.8 mmol/L. His carotid duplex ultrasound shows 75% right internal carotid artery stenosis. According to NICE guidance on TIA and stroke prevention, which of the following most accurately describes the most appropriate management?',
'Continue aspirin alone indefinitely and arrange annual carotid duplex follow-up',
'Switch to clopidogrel monotherapy and refer to stroke neurology for consideration of carotid endarterectomy given the 75% stenosis',
'Continue dual antiplatelet therapy with aspirin plus clopidogrel for 21 days then switch to clopidogrel alone; refer urgently for carotid endarterectomy or carotid artery stenting within 14 days of the TIA as 75% stenosis meets the surgical threshold; add high-intensity statin and smoking cessation support',
'Anticoagulate with apixaban as amaurosis fugax is a cardioembolic event requiring anticoagulation',
'Refer to neurosurgery for emergency carotid endarterectomy within 24 hours as 75% stenosis is a surgical emergency',
'C',
'CORRECT. This patient has had a TIA (amaurosis fugax — transient monocular visual loss from retinal ischaemia due to carotid embolism) with 75% ipsilateral carotid stenosis. NICE guidance on stroke prevention: (1) Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin + clopidogrel for 21 days following a TIA (updated NICE guidance following POINT/CHANCE trials), then clopidogrel monotherapy long-term; (2) Carotid endarterectomy (CEA) or carotid artery stenting (CAS): recommended within 14 days of the TIA for symptomatic carotid stenosis ≥50% — 75% stenosis clearly exceeds this threshold. CEA significantly reduces stroke risk in symptomatic significant stenosis (NNT approximately 6 for 5-year stroke prevention); (3) High-intensity statin therapy (atorvastatin 80 mg OD); (4) Blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg); (5) Smoking cessation. Aspirin alone is insufficient — dual antiplatelet and urgent CEA referral are required. NICE',
'Incorrect. Aspirin alone is insufficient following a TIA with significant ipsilateral carotid stenosis. NICE recommends dual antiplatelet therapy for 21 days, then clopidogrel monotherapy. Additionally, carotid endarterectomy referral is mandatory for 75% stenosis — aspirin alone without surgical referral is inadequate secondary prevention.',
'Incorrect. While clopidogrel monotherapy is the long-term antiplatelet agent of choice, the immediate post-TIA period (21 days) requires dual antiplatelet therapy per NICE. The referral for carotid intervention is correct but the antiplatelet regimen described in option B is incomplete.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Amaurosis fugax from carotid artery disease is an arterial (not cardioembolic) event. Unless AF or other cardioembolic source is identified, anticoagulation is not indicated — antiplatelet therapy is appropriate for large artery atherosclerotic TIA. Apixaban is not indicated here.',
'Incorrect. Carotid endarterectomy is urgent (within 14 days of TIA per NICE) but not a same-day emergency requiring neurosurgical admission within 24 hours. The emergency department already managed the acute phase. The GP should ensure the urgent CEA referral pathway is activated and antiplatelet/statin therapy is optimised.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 30-year-old woman presents to the GP with a 6-month history of headaches and intermittent blurring of vision. She is obese (BMI 38 kg/m²) and started the combined oral contraceptive pill 8 months ago. Neurological examination is normal except for bilateral papilloedema on fundoscopy. Her visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. CT head arranged urgently is reported as normal with no mass lesion. According to your knowledge of idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH), which of the following most accurately describes the diagnosis and the most appropriate immediate steps?',
'Reassure her that papilloedema with a normal CT is benign and requires no further investigation',
'Refer same-day to neurology or a specialist IIH service; arrange urgent MRI brain and MR venography to exclude venous sinus thrombosis; stop the COCP as it is a recognised risk factor for IIH; prescribe acetazolamide as first-line pharmacological treatment under specialist guidance',
'Prescribe high-dose oral dexamethasone immediately as steroid treatment is first-line for papilloedema',
'Refer to ophthalmology only for management of the papilloedema with optic nerve sheath fenestration',
'Refer to bariatric surgery as weight loss is the only treatment for IIH',
'B',
'CORRECT. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) — also called pseudotumour cerebri — predominantly affects young obese women and is characterised by: raised intracranial pressure without a space-occupying lesion, headaches, papilloedema, and visual obscurations. Risk factors include: obesity, COCP use, tetracycline antibiotics, vitamin A excess, steroid withdrawal. The normal CT head makes space-occupying lesion unlikely but does not exclude venous sinus thrombosis (which can cause identical presentation) — MRI with MR venography is required to exclude this. Management: (1) Urgent neurology referral for lumbar puncture (to confirm raised opening pressure >25 cmH2O and to provide diagnostic CSF); (2) Stop COCP — a recognised precipitant; (3) Weight loss (target 6–10% body weight reduction — most effective long-term treatment); (4) Acetazolamide (carbonic anhydrase inhibitor — reduces CSF production) as first-line pharmacological treatment under specialist guidance; (5) Regular visual field monitoring for optic nerve damage. NICE',
'Incorrect. Bilateral papilloedema is never benign and always requires urgent investigation. Papilloedema indicates raised intracranial pressure which, if sustained, causes progressive optic nerve damage and permanent visual loss. A normal CT does not exclude venous sinus thrombosis or IIH. Reassurance without further investigation is dangerous.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. High-dose corticosteroids are not first-line for IIH — in fact, steroid withdrawal is a recognised cause of IIH, and chronic steroid use can worsen obesity (a key risk factor). Acetazolamide is first-line pharmacological treatment under specialist guidance.',
'Incorrect. Optic nerve sheath fenestration (ONSF) is a surgical procedure used for IIH when vision is threatened and medical management has failed. It is not the appropriate first step — medical management (acetazolamide, weight loss) is first-line and ONSF is reserved for refractory cases with progressive visual loss.',
'Incorrect. Weight loss is the most effective long-term treatment for IIH and should be strongly encouraged — but bariatric surgery referral as the immediate first step is not appropriate. The acute management priorities are specialist neurology referral, MRI/MRV, lumbar puncture, COCP cessation, and acetazolamide initiation. Bariatric surgery may be considered later for patients with BMI >35 who fail medical management.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 48-year-old man with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) on hydroxychloroquine 400 mg OD for 3 years attends his GP for a routine review. He weighs 70 kg. His renal function shows an eGFR of 38 mL/min/1.73m² (previously 65 mL/min/1.73m² — now reduced due to lupus nephritis). He has no visual symptoms. Which of the following is the most important action regarding his hydroxychloroquine management?',
'Continue hydroxychloroquine at the same dose as renal impairment does not affect hydroxychloroquine dosing or toxicity risk',
'Increase hydroxychloroquine to 600 mg OD as lupus nephritis requires higher immunosuppression',
'Flag to the prescribing rheumatologist that his eGFR has fallen to 38 mL/min — reduced renal clearance increases hydroxychloroquine accumulation and retinal toxicity risk; dose reduction or increased monitoring frequency should be discussed; check the daily dose does not exceed 5 mg/kg actual body weight',
'Stop hydroxychloroquine immediately as it is absolutely contraindicated when eGFR falls below 45 mL/min',
'Switch to chloroquine as it has a safer renal profile than hydroxychloroquine in CKD',
'C',
'CORRECT. Hydroxychloroquine is partially renally excreted and its metabolites can accumulate in patients with reduced renal clearance, increasing the risk of retinal toxicity. Key considerations: (1) eGFR 38 mL/min represents moderate-severe CKD — the Royal College of Ophthalmologists guidance specifies that renal impairment is a risk factor for increased hydroxychloroquine toxicity and may require dose reduction or more frequent ophthalmological screening; (2) Daily dose safety: should not exceed 5 mg/kg actual body weight (this patient is 70 kg — maximum safe dose = 350 mg/day; he is on 400 mg OD which exceeds this); (3) The rheumatologist who initiated the drug must be informed of the change in renal function and the overdosing relative to body weight — a dose reduction discussion is needed; (4) Earlier onset of ophthalmological screening may be required. Stopping hydroxychloroquine completely is not the GP''s decision to make unilaterally for SLE management. NICE',
'Incorrect. Renal impairment DOES affect hydroxychloroquine pharmacokinetics — reduced eGFR reduces drug clearance, increasing plasma levels and metabolite accumulation, thereby increasing retinopathy risk. The dose may need to be reduced, not maintained.',
'Incorrect. Increasing the hydroxychloroquine dose in the context of renal impairment would further increase toxicity risk. Dose escalation decisions in SLE management are made by the rheumatologist — this is not appropriate for a GP to initiate unilaterally, and increasing dose in CKD is contraindicated.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Hydroxychloroquine is not absolutely contraindicated in eGFR <45 mL/min — it may be used with dose adjustment and increased monitoring. Abruptly stopping it without rheumatology input risks SLE disease flare, which can cause serious organ damage (renal, cardiac, neurological). The prescribing rheumatologist must be involved in this decision.',
'Incorrect. Chloroquine carries a HIGHER risk of retinal toxicity than hydroxychloroquine, not a lower risk. Switching to chloroquine in a patient with CKD would increase, not decrease, the risk of retinopathy. Chloroquine is rarely used in the UK for SLE due to its greater toxicity profile.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 58-year-old woman presents to the GP with a 3-week history of left eye pain, redness, and blurred vision. She describes the pain as deep and boring, worse at night, waking her from sleep. The pain radiates to her left temple and jaw. She has a history of rheumatoid arthritis managed with methotrexate. On examination, the left eye has a deep violaceous (purple-red) discolouration of the sclera in the temporal quadrant. The area is tender on gentle pressure. Visual acuity is 6/12 in the left eye. Which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol and ibuprofen gel and review in 1 week',
'Reassure that this is likely episcleritis which is self-limiting in patients with rheumatoid arthritis',
'Refer same-day to the emergency eye unit — scleritis is a sight-threatening emergency associated with systemic autoimmune disease requiring urgent specialist assessment and systemic anti-inflammatory treatment',
'Prescribe oral ibuprofen 400 mg TDS for 2 weeks and review as NSAIDs are first-line for scleritis',
'Increase methotrexate dose immediately as the scleritis represents a rheumatoid flare requiring enhanced immunosuppression',
'C',
'CORRECT. Scleritis is a serious, potentially sight-threatening inflammatory condition of the sclera strongly associated with systemic autoimmune diseases (rheumatoid arthritis, granulomatosis with polyangiitis, SLE, relapsing polychondritis). Distinguishing features from episcleritis: deep boring pain (worse at night, not relieved by topical vasoconstrictors), violaceous/purple discolouration of the deep scleral vessels (scleral oedema), tenderness on gentle globe pressure, and reduced visual acuity. Scleritis requires same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment because: (1) Necrotising scleritis can cause scleral thinning and perforation; (2) It may represent systemic vasculitis requiring urgent systemic investigation; (3) Treatment involves systemic NSAIDs, oral corticosteroids, or immunosuppressants (methotrexate, azathioprine) — these decisions require specialist input. Medication changes in this context must be coordinated with rheumatology and ophthalmology. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical chloramphenicol has no role in scleritis — this is an inflammatory, not infectious, condition. Ibuprofen gel applied topically to the face does not achieve therapeutic levels in the sclera. A 1-week delay for a patient with worsening visual acuity and deep boring pain is inappropriate.',
'Incorrect. Episcleritis is a mild, self-limiting inflammatory condition of the superficial episcleral tissue — it presents with a salmon-pink discolouration (not violaceous/purple), mild discomfort (not severe boring pain), no VA reduction, and no tenderness. This patient has a violaceous scleral discolouration with severe pain, visual loss, and nocturnal waking — these features indicate scleritis, not episcleritis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While oral NSAIDs are part of the treatment regimen for non-necrotising scleritis, prescribing them empirically in primary care without specialist assessment is insufficient. The visual acuity reduction to 6/12 indicates significant ocular involvement that requires specialist evaluation — ocular coherence tomography, slit-lamp biomicroscopy, and B-scan ultrasound may all be needed. The possibility of necrotising scleritis must be excluded by a specialist.',
'Incorrect. Adjusting methotrexate dose without rheumatology and ophthalmology input in the context of acute scleritis is inappropriate. The underlying cause needs specialist investigation — it may represent a vasculitic manifestation of RA requiring cyclophosphamide rather than simply increasing methotrexate. Unilateral medication dose escalation without specialist guidance could be harmful.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 7-year-old boy attends the GP with his mother, who has received a letter from the school nurse after a vision screening test. The letter states: "Right VA 6/6, Left VA 6/24. Refer to GP." On examination at the surgery, the right VA is 6/6 and the left VA is 6/24 with pinhole. He has no ptosis, no nystagmus, and extraocular movements are full. There is no obvious squint. Refraction at the optician shows left myopia of -1.75 DS. With correction, left VA remains 6/24. According to your knowledge of amblyopia (lazy eye) in children, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe glasses for the left eye and discharge as correction of the refractive error will resolve the visual deficit over time',
'Refer to paediatric ophthalmology urgently — reduced visual acuity that does not fully improve with optimal optical correction in a child suggests amblyopia requiring specialist assessment for patching therapy and to exclude other causes',
'Reassure the mother that a VA of 6/24 in a 7-year-old is within normal developmental range',
'Refer to optometry for glasses and review at the next school vision screening in 2 years',
'Arrange MRI brain to exclude a posterior visual pathway abnormality causing the reduced VA',
'B',
'CORRECT. Amblyopia (lazy eye) is reduced visual acuity in one or both eyes that is not fully correctable with optical correction, in the absence of structural eye disease — caused by abnormal visual experience during the critical period of visual development (typically up to 7–8 years of age). Causes: anisometropia (unequal refractive error — as in this case), strabismus (squint), and deprivation (ptosis, cataract). Key finding here: VA remains 6/24 even with pinhole (which corrects refractive error) — this means the VA deficit is NOT solely from uncorrected myopia, suggesting amblyopia. Referral to paediatric ophthalmology is urgent as: (1) The critical period for amblyopia treatment is time-sensitive — treatment after age 7–8 is less effective; (2) Patching therapy (occluding the better eye) is the mainstay of treatment; (3) Other causes of reduced VA must be excluded. This is a time-sensitive referral — delays risk permanent visual loss in the affected eye. NICE',
'Incorrect. Prescribing glasses alone is insufficient when VA remains 6/24 with pinhole correction. If the VA improved to 6/6 with optical correction, the deficit would be purely refractive. The persistent reduced VA with pinhole indicates amblyopia — glasses alone will not correct this without concurrent patching therapy under specialist supervision.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. A VA of 6/24 in a 7-year-old is NOT within normal developmental range — this represents clinically significant visual impairment. Normal visual acuity in a 7-year-old should be 6/6 or near. Reassurance without referral would deprive this child of time-sensitive treatment during the critical period.',
'Incorrect. A 2-year wait for re-screening is entirely inappropriate — by age 9, the visual cortex''s critical period for amblyopia treatment has largely closed. Delaying specialist referral by 2 years would likely result in permanent reduced vision in the left eye that would be difficult or impossible to treat.',
'Incorrect. MRI brain is not the appropriate immediate investigation for reduced VA in one eye in a child with an identified refractive error. The clinical picture is consistent with anisometropic amblyopia — MRI is not the priority. Paediatric ophthalmology referral for amblyopia assessment and treatment is the first step. MRI may be considered if the assessment reveals features inconsistent with amblyopia.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 65-year-old woman with no previous eye problems presents to her GP with a 2-week history of seeing flashing lights and a "shower of floaters" in her right eye. She woke up 3 days ago with a grey, crescent-shaped shadow in the lower visual field of her right eye. Her vision has not changed significantly. Her VA is 6/9 in the right eye and 6/6 in the left. She is highly myopic (-9.00 DS). According to NICE guidance on retinal detachment, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Reassure her that floaters and flashes are common with high myopia and arrange optician review in 2 weeks',
'Refer same-day to the emergency eye unit — the combination of flashes, floaters, and a visual field defect (grey shadow) in a highly myopic patient is consistent with retinal detachment, which is a surgical emergency',
'Prescribe a short course of topical prednisolone for posterior vitreous detachment-related inflammation',
'Arrange urgent OCT scanning in primary care within 48 hours to assess for retinal tear before hospital referral',
'Refer routinely to ophthalmology within 6 weeks for assessment of the new floaters',
'B',
'CORRECT. This presentation — flashes, floaters, and a crescent-shaped visual field shadow — in a highly myopic patient is a retinal detachment until proven otherwise and constitutes a same-day ophthalmic emergency. High myopia is a major risk factor for retinal detachment (thin, stretched peripheral retina prone to tears). The clinical sequence: (1) Posterior vitreous detachment (PVD) — vitreous gel separates from retina causing flashes (traction on photoreceptors) and floaters (vitreous opacities); (2) Retinal tear — PVD can create a horseshoe/U-shaped retinal tear; (3) Retinal detachment — fluid enters through the tear, lifting the retina. The grey shadow in the lower field suggests fluid has accumulated inferiorly (the tear is typically superior — gravity causes superior fluid to accumulate inferiorly producing an inferior field defect). Time is critical: if the macula is still attached (macula-on detachment — VA 6/9 suggests this), urgent surgery achieves much better visual outcomes than delayed surgery after macular detachment. Same-day emergency referral is mandatory. NICE',
'Incorrect. Floaters and flashes in a highly myopic patient with a new visual field defect must not be dismissed as benign. While floaters alone in a non-myopic patient without field defects may warrant urgent (rather than emergency) assessment, the combination of flashes + floaters + shadow field defect in a highly myopic patient requires same-day emergency ophthalmology referral.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Posterior vitreous detachment does not cause significant intraocular inflammation requiring topical steroids. More critically, topical steroids have no role in retinal detachment. The visual field shadow indicates detachment has occurred — this requires surgical treatment, not topical anti-inflammatory therapy.',
'Incorrect. OCT in primary care is not routinely available and would not be an appropriate substitute for emergency ophthalmology assessment. The combination of field defect with flashes and floaters requires same-day assessment by an ophthalmologist with slit-lamp examination and indirect ophthalmoscopy — not delayed OCT imaging.',
'Incorrect. A routine 6-week referral is completely inappropriate for a patient with a visual field defect consistent with retinal detachment. A 6-week delay would result in macular detachment (if not already present), permanent severe central vision loss, and a significantly worse surgical outcome. Same-day emergency referral is mandatory.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 75-year-old man with hypertension and type 2 diabetes presents to his GP with sudden, painless, complete visual loss in his right eye of 2 hours duration. He describes it as a "black curtain" affecting the entire right eye with no light perception. He had no preceding flashes or floaters. His right afferent pupillary defect is present (RAPD positive). His visual acuity is no light perception (NLP) in the right eye and 6/6 in the left. On fundoscopy, the right retina appears pale and oedematous with a cherry red spot at the macula. According to your knowledge of central retinal artery occlusion, which of the following is correct regarding the management and the significance of the cherry red spot?',
'The cherry red spot is a normal variant in diabetic patients; prescribe aspirin and arrange GP review in 1 week',
'The cherry red spot results from the relatively transparent fovea revealing the underlying choroidal circulation against a pale ischaemic retina; this is central retinal artery occlusion — refer immediately to the emergency eye unit and the stroke team as this is a vascular emergency equivalent to a stroke',
'Administer IV tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) immediately in the GP surgery as CRAO responds to thrombolysis',
'The cherry red spot indicates retinitis pigmentosa; refer routinely to ophthalmology',
'Reassure the patient; cherry red spots always resolve spontaneously within 24 hours',
'B',
'CORRECT. Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) is the ophthalmic equivalent of a stroke and is caused by thromboembolism of the central retinal artery, causing sudden complete painless visual loss with no light perception. The fundoscopic finding of: pale, oedematous retina with a cherry red spot at the fovea is pathognomonic — the cherry red spot appears because: (1) The entire retina becomes pale and opaque from ischaemia (retinal ganglion cell death begins within minutes); (2) The fovea (foveola) has no ganglion cell layer and relies on the underlying choroidal circulation (not the retinal artery) — it therefore remains transparent and shows the underlying red choroidal vessels against the pale ischaemic retina. CRAO is associated with carotid artery disease, AF, hypertension, and diabetes — all present here. Management: (1) Same-day emergency ophthalmology AND TIA/stroke team referral (urgent carotid imaging, echocardiography, and secondary prevention — the 90-day stroke risk after CRAO is approximately 10%); (2) Ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, and inhalation of carbon dioxide/oxygen mix may be tried but evidence is limited; (3) Visual prognosis is poor if NLP at presentation. NICE/RCOphth',
'Incorrect. A cherry red spot in the context of sudden complete visual loss, pale retina, and RAPD is not a normal variant — it is pathognomonic of CRAO. Aspirin alone and GP review in 1 week would miss a vascular emergency and fail to initiate secondary stroke prevention.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. IV tPA in the GP surgery is not appropriate — it requires hospital administration with specialist oversight, imaging to exclude contraindications, and carries risks of haemorrhage. There is limited evidence for IV tPA in CRAO and it is not standard primary care management. The GP''s role is immediate emergency transfer.',
'Incorrect. Retinitis pigmentosa causes progressive peripheral visual field loss with a "bone spicule" pattern of pigmentary change, waxy disc pallor, and attenuated vessels — not a sudden onset of complete visual loss with a cherry red spot. These are entirely different conditions.',
'Incorrect. Cherry red spots from CRAO do NOT resolve spontaneously — retinal ischaemia progresses within minutes to hours, causing permanent ganglion cell death. The cherry red spot gradually disappears as the retinal oedema settles and the fovea becomes atrophic, but this does not represent recovery. The visual loss from CRAO is typically permanent.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 34-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 3-day history of a painless, slow-growing white lesion on the lower right eyelid margin. It is approximately 3 mm, round, with a central umbilication (central dimple). She is HIV-positive and not yet on antiretroviral therapy. Her CD4 count is 140 cells/µL. She has similar small lesions on her trunk and arms. According to your knowledge of molluscum contagiosum and immunocompromised patients, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Prescribe topical imiquimod cream to all lesions as this is first-line treatment for molluscum contagiosum in all patients',
'Reassure her that molluscum contagiosum is self-limiting and will resolve without treatment within 18 months',
'Refer to ophthalmology for assessment of the eyelid lesion; initiate or optimise antiretroviral therapy urgently as extensive molluscum in an HIV patient with CD4 140 cells/µL indicates severe immunodeficiency requiring ART; refer to HIV medicine team',
'Prescribe oral aciclovir for 7 days as molluscum contagiosum is caused by herpes simplex virus',
'Apply liquid nitrogen cryotherapy to all lesions in the GP surgery at this appointment',
'C',
'CORRECT. Molluscum contagiosum (MC) is caused by a poxvirus (not herpes simplex) and presents as pearly umbilicated papules. In immunocompetent patients, MC is self-limiting. However, in HIV-positive patients with severe immunodeficiency (CD4 <200 cells/µL), MC can be: extensive (hundreds of lesions), large (>5 mm — giant molluscum), persistent, and recalcitrant to local treatment. Key points: (1) Eyelid molluscum: lesions on or near the eyelid margin shed viral particles into the conjunctival sac, causing a chronic follicular conjunctivitis — ophthalmology assessment is required; (2) ART is the most effective treatment — immune restoration dramatically reduces molluscum burden; (3) CD4 140 cells/µL = severe immunodeficiency requiring urgent ART initiation by the HIV team; (4) Local treatments (cryotherapy, curettage, imiquimod) can be used but are less effective without immune reconstitution. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical imiquimod is not evidence-based as first-line for all molluscum contagiosum. For immunocompetent patients, watchful waiting is often appropriate. For HIV patients with low CD4 counts, immune reconstitution via ART is the priority treatment. Imiquimod alone in severe immunodeficiency is unlikely to control extensive molluscum.',
'Incorrect. While MC is self-limiting in immunocompetent individuals (typically resolving in 12–18 months), in patients with severe HIV immunodeficiency (CD4 140 cells/µL), MC will NOT self-resolve without immune reconstitution. Untreated, it becomes increasingly extensive and disfiguring. Reassurance without ART initiation is inappropriate.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Molluscum contagiosum is caused by the Molluscum contagiosum virus (MCV) — a poxvirus — not herpes simplex virus. Aciclovir has no activity against poxviruses and would be ineffective.',
'Incorrect. Cryotherapy to lesions in the GP surgery at the same appointment is not the priority management. For multiple extensive lesions in a severely immunocompromised patient, cryotherapy alone without addressing the underlying immune deficiency is insufficient and would need to be repeated indefinitely. Furthermore, eyelid lesions require ophthalmology assessment before any local treatment near the eye.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 40-year-old woman presents to her GP requesting advice about laser eye surgery (LASIK). She has myopia of -6.00 DS bilaterally with astigmatism (-1.50 DC). She wears contact lenses daily. She has been told she is a suitable candidate by a private laser eye surgery clinic. She asks her GP for an informed opinion on the risks and who should not have the procedure. According to your knowledge of refractive surgery, which of the following most accurately describes a significant contraindication to LASIK?',
'Myopia of -6.00 DS — high myopia is an absolute contraindication to all laser eye surgery',
'Age over 30 — LASIK should only be performed in patients under 30 when the refractive prescription has been stable',
'Keratoconus or forme fruste keratoconus — progressive corneal ectasia is an absolute contraindication to LASIK as ablation of a structurally weak cornea accelerates ectasia and causes severe irregular astigmatism',
'Contact lens wear — patients who wear contact lenses cannot safely undergo LASIK at any time',
'Stable myopia — if the prescription has been stable for 2 years LASIK is risk-free',
'C',
'CORRECT. Keratoconus (progressive corneal ectasia — thinning and forward bulging of the cornea) and its subclinical form (forme fruste keratoconus) are absolute contraindications to LASIK. LASIK involves creating a corneal flap and ablating stromal tissue — in a cornea that is already biomechanically weak (keratoconus), this causes further structural compromise, leading to progressive post-LASIK ectasia: severe irregular astigmatism, progressive visual deterioration, and potential corneal transplant requirement. Pre-operative screening with corneal topography and tomography (Pentacam/Scheimpflug imaging) is mandatory to exclude keratoconus before LASIK. Other absolute contraindications: severe dry eye syndrome, autoimmune conditions affecting the eye, corneal scarring. Relative contraindications: high myopia (>-10.00 DS), thin corneas (residual stromal bed <250 microns post-ablation), unstable refraction. NICE',
'Incorrect. High myopia (-6.00 DS) is not an absolute contraindication to LASIK — it is a relative consideration depending on corneal thickness. Myopia up to -10.00 DS can often be treated with LASIK if corneal thickness is adequate. The procedure is frequently performed for myopia in this range.',
'Incorrect. Age over 30 is not a contraindication to LASIK. The requirement is that the refractive prescription has been stable for at least 1–2 years — not that the patient must be under 30. LASIK is routinely performed in adults of all ages up to approximately 55 years (after which presbyopia becomes a greater consideration).',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Contact lens wear is not a contraindication to LASIK — but contact lenses must be discontinued before surgery (soft lenses 2–4 weeks before, rigid lenses 4–6 weeks before) to allow corneal shape to normalise before pre-operative measurements are taken. Continued contact lens wear is not a reason to refuse LASIK permanently.',
'Incorrect. No surgical procedure is ever "risk-free." LASIK risks include: dry eye syndrome (common), night vision disturbances (halos, starbursts — common), undercorrection or overcorrection requiring enhancement, flap complications, infection, and the rare but devastating post-LASIK ectasia (particularly if keratoconus is missed pre-operatively). The patient should be counselled about these risks before consenting.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 62-year-old man presents to his GP with a 4-week history of drooping of his right upper eyelid. He first noticed it was worse at the end of the day and improves after sleep. He has also noticed intermittent double vision, particularly when looking upward. He has no pain, no headache, and no changes in pupil size. His past medical history includes a 30 pack-year smoking history. On examination, right ptosis is confirmed, with partial improvement with ice pack application (ice test positive). According to your knowledge of myasthenia gravis and ptosis causes in primary care, which of the following most accurately identifies the most likely diagnosis and the most important investigation to arrange?',
'Mechanical ptosis from dermatochalasis; refer to oculoplastic surgery for blepharoplasty',
'Horner syndrome; arrange urgent CT chest to exclude apical lung tumour (Pancoast tumour) as a cause of the sympathetic chain disruption',
'Third nerve palsy with pupil involvement; arrange same-day CT brain to exclude posterior communicating artery aneurysm',
'Myasthenia gravis; arrange acetylcholine receptor antibody (AChR-Ab) blood test and refer to neurology urgently; the variable/fatigable ptosis and positive ice test are characteristic',
'Senile ptosis; reassure and refer to oculoplastic surgery for routine ptosis repair',
'D',
'CORRECT. The clinical features are characteristic of ocular or generalised myasthenia gravis (MG): (1) Fatigable ptosis — worse at end of day, improves after rest/sleep (fatigue of the levator palpebrae superioris due to acetylcholine receptor autoantibody-mediated neuromuscular junction dysfunction); (2) Positive ice test — applying ice to the eyelid for 2 minutes improves ptosis in MG (cold temperature enhances acetylcholinesterase activity); (3) Diplopia — intermittent extraocular muscle weakness; (4) No pupil involvement (distinguishes from third nerve palsy). Investigations: (1) AChR-Ab — positive in approximately 50% of purely ocular MG and 85% of generalised MG; (2) Anti-MuSK antibodies if AChR-Ab negative; (3) CT chest — thymic abnormality (thymoma or thymic hyperplasia) is present in approximately 15% of MG patients; (4) Neurophysiology (repetitive nerve stimulation, single-fibre EMG). Urgent neurology referral is required. NICE',
'Incorrect. Dermatochalasis (excess upper eyelid skin) causes mechanical ptosis — it is bilateral, does not vary with fatigue, and is not associated with diplopia. It is a cosmetic/functional issue in older patients without the systemic features of MG. There is no ice test positivity in mechanical ptosis.',
'Incorrect. Horner syndrome causes partial ptosis (2–3 mm), miosis (small pupil), and anhidrosis — it does NOT cause fatigable ptosis or diplopia. In Horner syndrome, the ptosis is constant (not variable), and a CT chest would be important to exclude Pancoast tumour. However, the absent miosis, variable fatigable ptosis, and ice test positivity point to MG rather than Horner syndrome.',
'Incorrect. Third nerve (CN III) palsy causes complete ptosis with diplopia and, critically, pupil involvement (dilated fixed pupil) in compressive lesions (posterior communicating artery aneurysm — a neurosurgical emergency). The absence of pupil changes in this patient makes compressive third nerve palsy less likely. Moreover, fatigable ptosis and positive ice test are specific to MG.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Senile (involutional) ptosis is caused by dehiscence or disinsertion of the levator aponeurosis — it is typically bilateral, does not vary with fatigue, and is not associated with diplopia. The ice test would not be positive in senile ptosis. The clinical features here — fatigable ptosis with diplopia and positive ice test — are specific to myasthenia gravis and require urgent neurology referral, not routine oculoplastic referral.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 50-year-old man presents to his GP with recurrent episodes of red, painful eyes occurring approximately 4 times per year for the past 3 years. Each episode is unilateral, lasts 4–6 weeks, and resolves spontaneously. He has a history of lower back pain and morning stiffness that improved with exercise over the past 5 years. He was told by his physiotherapist he might have "inflammatory back disease." He has no skin rash and no bowel symptoms. According to your knowledge of extra-articular manifestations of spondyloarthropathy, which of the following most accurately identifies the diagnosis and most important systemic investigation?',
'Recurrent viral conjunctivitis; prescribe topical antihistamine eye drops and advise on eye hygiene',
'Recurrent anterior uveitis associated with seronegative spondyloarthropathy; the most important investigation is HLA-B27 testing alongside inflammatory markers and rheumatology referral for the underlying spinal disease',
'Recurrent episcleritis from rosacea; prescribe oral doxycycline and topical metronidazole',
'Recurrent herpes simplex uveitis; prescribe long-term oral aciclovir prophylaxis',
'Bilateral anterior uveitis from sarcoidosis; arrange chest X-ray and serum ACE measurement',
'B',
'CORRECT. Recurrent unilateral anterior uveitis (iritis) occurring multiple times per year is the most common extra-articular manifestation of seronegative spondyloarthropathies (ankylosing spondylitis, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, IBD-associated arthritis). This patient has: (1) Recurrent unilateral acute anterior uveitis — typical pattern for HLA-B27-associated uveitis (alternating sides, acute onset, typically resolves within 4–6 weeks); (2) Inflammatory back pain and stiffness (improving with exercise — characteristic of axial spondyloarthropathy vs mechanical back pain which worsens with exercise); (3) No preceding viral illness or STI (distinguishing from reactive arthritis). Most important investigation: HLA-B27 (positive in approximately 90% of ankylosing spondylitis; positive in approximately 50–70% of acute anterior uveitis overall). Rheumatology referral for investigation of the possible axial spondyloarthropathy (sacroiliac joint imaging — MRI). Ophthalmology referral for each acute episode of uveitis. NICE',
'Incorrect. Viral conjunctivitis causes bilateral watery discharge, gritty sensation, follicular conjunctivitis — it does not cause recurrent unilateral episodes of red painful eye with photophobia lasting 4–6 weeks. Antihistamine drops have no role. The duration and recurrence pattern are inconsistent with viral conjunctivitis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Episcleritis from rosacea occurs in the context of rosacea skin disease (facial erythema, telangiectasia, rhinophyma). There is no mention of rosacea in this patient. Episcleritis causes a salmon-pink discolouration with mild discomfort — not the severe recurrent painful red eye described here.',
'Incorrect. Herpes simplex uveitis is typically a unilateral recurrent uveitis, but it is associated with corneal dendrites and corneal scarring from HSV keratitis. It does not typically cause inflammatory back pain. HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy-associated uveitis is far more consistent with this clinical picture.',
'Incorrect. Sarcoid uveitis is typically bilateral, chronic, or insidious in onset — not the acute recurrent unilateral pattern described here. Sarcoidosis would also be associated with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, skin lesions, or other systemic features. HLA-B27 spondyloarthropathy is the far more consistent diagnosis for recurrent unilateral anterior uveitis with inflammatory back disease.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 55-year-old man presents to his GP. He was recently reviewed by his optician who found: normal visual acuity bilaterally, normal IOP bilaterally, but an unusual appearance of the optic discs with pale temporal disc margins bilaterally. The optician''s letter states: "Temporal disc pallor bilaterally — please refer for further assessment." The patient has no visual symptoms but does complain of headaches and some difficulty reading small print. He has no history of eye disease or neurological conditions. According to your knowledge of optic disc pallor and its differential diagnosis, which of the following is the most appropriate management?',
'Reassure the patient that temporal disc pallor is a normal variant in middle-aged patients',
'Prescribe glasses for presbyopia as the reading difficulty is age-related and the disc pallor is incidental',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology or neurology — bilateral optic disc pallor indicates previous or ongoing optic nerve damage; differential diagnosis includes multiple sclerosis, compressive optic neuropathy, hereditary optic neuropathy, and chronic glaucoma; urgent investigation including VEPs, MRI brain and orbits, and formal visual field testing is required',
'Arrange urgent CT brain as bilateral disc pallor always indicates a space-occupying intracranial lesion requiring neurosurgical assessment',
'Refer routinely to optometry for repeat assessment as disc appearance varies with individual anatomy',
'C',
'CORRECT. Optic disc pallor (optic atrophy) indicates loss of nerve fibres from the optic nerve — it represents end-organ damage that has occurred from various causes. Bilateral optic disc pallor requires urgent investigation as it can indicate: (1) Multiple sclerosis — bilateral optic neuritis (previous or current); (2) Compressive optic neuropathy — pituitary tumour (bitemporal field defect), meningioma, craniopharyngioma; (3) Leber hereditary optic neuropathy (LHON) — mitochondrial genetic condition causing bilateral optic atrophy in young men; (4) Toxic/nutritional optic neuropathy (B12 deficiency, tobacco-alcohol amblyopia, drug toxicity); (5) Chronic glaucoma with significant nerve fibre loss. Investigation: urgent referral to ophthalmology or neurology for visual field testing (formal Humphrey or Goldmann), MRI brain and orbits (with gadolinium), visual evoked potentials (VEPs), and blood tests (B12, folate, mitochondrial DNA if LHON suspected). NICE',
'Incorrect. Bilateral optic disc pallor is NEVER a normal variant — it indicates prior optic nerve damage from one of several potentially serious conditions. Reassurance without investigation risks missing MS, pituitary tumour, or other treatable conditions.',
'Incorrect. Presbyopia (age-related loss of near accommodation) is common and requires glasses — but it is unrelated to optic disc pallor. Dismissing the disc pallor as incidental and prescribing reading glasses misses the urgent need to investigate the cause of bilateral optic atrophy.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While a space-occupying lesion is in the differential for bilateral optic disc pallor, not ALL cases require emergency neurosurgical assessment — some causes (MS, LHON, toxic) do not require CT or surgery. MRI brain and orbits (not CT) is the preferred imaging, and this should be arranged through the specialist referral rather than as an emergency direct GP request in a patient without acute neurological symptoms.',
'Incorrect. Repeat optometry assessment of bilateral optic disc pallor is not appropriate — this is a clinical finding that requires specialist investigation. Optometric re-examination would not change the need for urgent referral. The finding has been noted by an optician who has appropriately referred to the GP — routine re-optometry before specialist referral introduces unnecessary delay.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP is reviewing a 68-year-old woman with a 5-year history of open-angle glaucoma managed by the hospital eye service with latanoprost 0.005% eye drops OD (one drop each eye at night). She attends the GP surgery for a routine review and mentions she has developed a persistent dry cough over the past 3 months since her GP started ramipril 5 mg OD for hypertension. Her glaucoma is well-controlled. Her blood pressure today is 128/76 mmHg. According to NICE guidance on hypertension treatment in patients with glaucoma on topical beta-blockers, which of the following is the most appropriate advice regarding the glaucoma drops and the cough?',
'Stop latanoprost as prostaglandin analogues cause dry cough as a class effect',
'The dry cough is caused by ramipril (ACE inhibitor) not latanoprost; switch ramipril to an ARB (losartan or candesartan); latanoprost does not cause cough and should be continued',
'Stop ramipril and start topical timolol eye drops to treat both glaucoma and hypertension simultaneously',
'The cough is caused by latanoprost; no change to ramipril is needed; increase the frequency of latanoprost to BD to speed up resolution of the cough',
'Prescribe topical timolol for glaucoma alongside ramipril as combining a topical beta-blocker with an ACE inhibitor reduces cough risk',
'B',
'CORRECT. The dry cough in this patient is caused by ramipril (an ACE inhibitor) — not latanoprost. ACE inhibitor-induced cough results from bradykinin accumulation in the respiratory tract (bradykinin is normally metabolised by ACE). It affects approximately 10–15% of patients and is more common in women. Latanoprost is a prostaglandin analogue — it does NOT cause cough. Its ocular side effects include: increased iris pigmentation, eyelash growth (hypertrichosis), periocular skin darkening, and conjunctival hyperaemia — not cough. Management: switch ramipril to an angiotensin receptor blocker (ARB — losartan, candesartan, valsartan) which provides equivalent antihypertensive and renoprotective effects without the bradykinin-mediated cough side effect. Important note: topical beta-blockers (timolol) used for glaucoma are SYSTEMICALLY ABSORBED and can exacerbate asthma, cause bradycardia, and mask hypoglycaemia — they should NOT be started in a patient with respiratory conditions. NICE',
'Incorrect. Latanoprost (a prostaglandin analogue) does not cause dry cough. Its side effects are ocular (iris pigmentation, lash growth, conjunctival hyperaemia) and local skin effects. Stopping latanoprost would compromise glaucoma control without addressing the cough.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Topical timolol (a non-selective beta-blocker) is used for glaucoma but is significantly absorbed systemically through the nasolacrimal duct. In patients with respiratory disease or COPD risk, it can cause bronchospasm and worsening of respiratory symptoms. Stopping ramipril and starting topical timolol would not treat hypertension adequately (topical timolol provides negligible systemic antihypertensive effect) and could cause respiratory problems.',
'Incorrect. The cough is caused by ramipril, not latanoprost. Latanoprost does not cause cough and increasing its frequency would have no effect on the cough. Twice-daily dosing of latanoprost is also not the standard regimen — latanoprost is administered once daily at night.',
'Incorrect. Topical timolol is systemically absorbed and can worsen respiratory conditions including cough and bronchospasm. Adding timolol in a patient who already has a cough from ACE inhibitor use could exacerbate respiratory symptoms. The correct action is to switch the ACE inhibitor to an ARB, not to add a systemic beta-blocker through the ophthalmic route.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 72-year-old man with a background of ischaemic heart disease and type 2 diabetes presents to the GP with a sudden onset of complete painless visual loss in his right eye that occurred 45 minutes ago. He is currently taking aspirin, atorvastatin, bisoprolol, ramipril, and metformin. His blood pressure is 164/94 mmHg. An Amsler grid test confirms a complete right visual field blackout. He has previously had a similar episode 6 months ago that resolved within 10 minutes (presumed TIA). Which of the following most accurately identifies the correct referral pathway and the significance of his previous episode?',
'This is likely vitreous haemorrhage — refer routinely to ophthalmology within 2 weeks and increase aspirin dose',
'Refer same-day to ophthalmology and the stroke/TIA service — the current episode with complete visual loss lasting 45 minutes may be CRAO or prolonged amaurosis fugax; the previous 10-minute episode 6 months ago represents a prior TIA that was not investigated and constitutes a missed opportunity for stroke prevention; urgent carotid imaging and cardioembolic workup are required',
'Prescribe heparin immediately in the GP surgery to prevent propagation of presumed retinal artery thrombus',
'Arrange urgent CT brain before any ophthalmology referral to exclude haemorrhagic stroke',
'The current episode is likely migraine with aura — prescribe a triptan and review in 2 weeks',
'B',
'CORRECT. This patient has had two episodes of acute monocular visual loss: (1) The previous 10-minute episode 6 months ago was amaurosis fugax — a transient ischaemic attack (TIA) affecting the retinal circulation, almost certainly from a carotid or cardioembolic source; (2) The current episode (45 minutes duration, still ongoing) represents either a prolonged TIA or established central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO). Both require emergency assessment. The previous episode represents a missed TIA — after which NICE mandates: specialist TIA assessment within 24 hours, dual antiplatelet therapy, statin, carotid imaging, and secondary prevention. Had this been done, the current episode might have been prevented. Current management: same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment (to determine if this is CRAO) AND stroke team assessment (secondary prevention, carotid imaging, echocardiography). This patient has multiple risk factors for cardioembolic or atherosclerotic disease and is on aspirin alone — dual antiplatelet or anticoagulation decisions need specialist review. NICE',
'Incorrect. Vitreous haemorrhage causes painless visual loss but typically presents as floaters, shadows, or "red haze" — not complete blackout of the entire visual field. Vitreous haemorrhage is not typically sudden and complete. Routine 2-week referral for complete sudden visual loss is inappropriate.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. IV heparin is not administered in primary care and is not the standard immediate management for suspected retinal artery occlusion. The GP''s role is emergency transfer — not pharmacological intervention in the surgery. Heparin administration requires specialist oversight and monitoring.',
'Incorrect. CT brain is used for suspected haemorrhagic stroke (sudden onset severe headache, neurological deficit, altered consciousness). Monocular visual loss from retinal or optic nerve ischaemia does not require CT brain as the immediate investigation — ophthalmology and stroke team assessment take priority. CT brain would delay the emergency eye assessment.',
'Incorrect. Migraine with visual aura (scintillating scotoma, fortification spectra) typically causes bilateral positive visual phenomena (zigzag lines, flashing lights) in both visual fields — it is usually associated with headache and typically lasts 20–60 minutes. Complete painless monocular blackout in a patient with vascular risk factors and a previous similar episode is far more consistent with vascular ocular pathology (amaurosis fugax, CRAO) than migraine.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 45-year-old woman with no previous medical history presents to her GP with 3 months of intermittent horizontal diplopia (double vision), worse when looking to the right. She also describes headaches on waking and has gained 10 kg in weight over the past year. She has no eye pain. On examination, her right eye fails to abduct fully (limited lateral gaze to the right), consistent with a right sixth nerve palsy. Her blood pressure is 158/96 mmHg. Which of the following most accurately describes the significance of a sixth nerve palsy and the most appropriate management?',
'A sixth nerve palsy is always benign and self-limiting — prescribe a prism for the diplopia and review in 6 months',
'A sixth nerve palsy (abducens nerve palsy) is a false localising sign of raised intracranial pressure — urgent MRI brain with gadolinium is required to exclude a space-occupying lesion, venous sinus thrombosis, or idiopathic intracranial hypertension, particularly given the morning headaches and weight gain',
'The sixth nerve palsy is caused by her hypertension — control blood pressure and review in 3 months',
'Refer urgently to orthoptics for prism fitting as sixth nerve palsy in adults is always caused by ischaemia and resolves within 3 months',
'Arrange a random blood glucose immediately as diabetic mononeuropathy is the most common cause of sixth nerve palsy and management requires glycaemic control only',
'B',
'CORRECT. The sixth cranial nerve (abducens) has the longest intracranial course and is most vulnerable to raised intracranial pressure (ICP) — it is a classic "false localising sign" (the palsy does not indicate the site of the lesion but reflects raised ICP compressing the nerve against the petrous bone). This patient has: (1) Sixth nerve palsy; (2) Morning headaches (morning headaches are characteristic of raised ICP — CSF pressure peaks after prolonged recumbency); (3) Significant weight gain; (4) Hypertension. This presentation is highly suspicious for idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) — predominantly affecting obese women of childbearing age. Other causes of sixth nerve palsy: hypertensive mononeuropathy, diabetic mononeuropathy, multiple sclerosis, brainstem tumour, cavernous sinus lesion, meningitis. Urgent MRI brain with gadolinium (and MR venography to exclude venous sinus thrombosis) is mandatory before assuming a benign aetiology. NICE',
'Incorrect. A sixth nerve palsy is not always benign and self-limiting — it can indicate raised intracranial pressure from a space-occupying lesion, venous sinus thrombosis, or IIH. A 6-month review with a prism for diplopia without investigating the underlying cause could miss a serious neurological condition.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. While hypertension is a risk factor for microvascular mononeuropathy causing sixth nerve palsy, attributing the palsy solely to hypertension without investigating other causes (particularly raised ICP given the morning headaches and weight gain) is premature. Urgent MRI is required regardless of the blood pressure findings.',
'Incorrect. While diabetic mononeuropathy is a cause of sixth nerve palsy, it is a diagnosis of exclusion — other causes (particularly raised ICP) must be excluded first with urgent MRI. Glycaemic control alone without neuroimaging would miss potentially treatable causes of raised ICP.',
'Incorrect. Ischaemic sixth nerve palsy (from hypertension or diabetes) does resolve within 2–3 months in most cases, but this diagnosis cannot be made without first excluding structural causes with neuroimaging. Referring directly to orthoptics without neuroimaging investigation would miss space-occupying lesions, venous sinus thrombosis, and IIH — all potentially life-threatening.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 32-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes presents to her GP. Her diabetic eye screening result this year shows: "R0 — no retinopathy. M0 — no maculopathy." However, she asks her GP about protecting her eye health long-term. She smokes 10 cigarettes per day, her HbA1c is 68 mmol/mol, her blood pressure is 138/86 mmHg, and her total cholesterol is 5.2 mmol/L. Her eGFR is 72 mL/min/1.73m². According to NICE guidance on diabetic eye disease prevention, which of the following represents the most complete preventive strategy?',
'Annual retinal photography is the only preventive measure needed as she has no current retinopathy',
'Prescribe high-dose vitamin C and E antioxidant supplements as these have been shown to prevent diabetic retinopathy',
'Optimise glycaemic control (target HbA1c ≤53 mmol/mol for type 1 diabetes), achieve blood pressure control (target <130/80 mmHg in diabetes with complications risk), offer smoking cessation support, continue annual eye screening, and consider statin therapy given her cardiovascular risk profile; counsel her that intensive risk factor control significantly reduces her lifetime risk of developing sight-threatening retinopathy',
'No further action beyond annual screening as she has no current retinopathy — prevention is not possible once type 1 diabetes is diagnosed',
'Refer to endocrinology for insulin pump therapy as this is the only intervention proven to prevent diabetic retinopathy',
'C',
'CORRECT. Diabetic retinopathy prevention rests on comprehensive modifiable risk factor control. Evidence-based preventive strategies for this patient: (1) Glycaemic control: HbA1c 68 mmol/mol exceeds the target of ≤53 mmol/mol for type 1 diabetes (DCCT/EDIC trial demonstrated that intensive glycaemic control reduces retinopathy risk by 76% — the most powerful intervention); (2) Blood pressure: target <130/80 mmHg in diabetes — ramipril/ACE inhibitors have additional renoprotective and retinopathy-protective effects; (3) Smoking cessation: smoking accelerates diabetic vascular disease; (4) Annual retinal photography screening: essential for early detection; (5) Lipid control: statins reduce cardiovascular risk; some evidence for benefit in DR (ACCORD trial); (6) Fenofibrate: has specific evidence for slowing retinopathy progression in type 2 diabetes (FIELD/ACCORD-Eye trials) — less evidence in type 1 but may be considered. NICE',
'Incorrect. Annual retinal photography is essential but is only one component of diabetic eye disease prevention. The most important preventive interventions are glycaemic optimisation and blood pressure control — this patient''s HbA1c of 68 (above target) and BP 138/86 (above target) both require active management.',
'Incorrect. High-dose vitamin C and E antioxidant supplements are NOT recommended by NICE for diabetic retinopathy prevention. Large-scale trials (HOPE, REACT) did not demonstrate a protective effect of antioxidant vitamins on diabetic retinopathy. Supplementation with vitamins C and E is not part of the NICE-recommended prevention strategy.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Prevention is absolutely possible in type 1 diabetes — the DCCT trial clearly demonstrated that intensive glycaemic control (reducing HbA1c) reduced the risk of developing retinopathy by 76% and slowed progression in those with existing mild retinopathy. Telling a patient that prevention is not possible is inaccurate and denies her the motivation to optimise her risk factors.',
'Incorrect. Insulin pump therapy (continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion — CSII) can improve glycaemic control and reduce HbA1c variability — which may reduce retinopathy risk through better glucose control. However, it is not the ONLY intervention proven to prevent retinopathy, and referral to endocrinology for pump therapy without addressing BP, smoking, and lipids is an incomplete preventive strategy.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 29-year-old man presents to his GP reporting that he sees halos around lights at night and experiences "rainbow rings" around streetlights. He has no pain, no redness, and his visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. He wears contact lenses. The symptoms are worse after prolonged contact lens wear and improve when he removes his lenses and rests his eyes. He has never had glaucoma testing. According to your knowledge of the differential diagnosis of haloes and the most appropriate investigation, which of the following is the most appropriate first step?',
'Refer immediately to the emergency eye unit as halos are always caused by acute angle-closure glaucoma',
'Reassure him that halos are a normal phenomenon related to contact lens wear and no investigation is needed',
'Measure the intraocular pressure in primary care using a non-contact tonometer or arrange urgent optician review to check IOP and assess for glaucoma; also assess contact lens fit and material as poorly fitting or overworn lenses cause corneal oedema producing halos',
'Prescribe anti-glaucoma drops (latanoprost) empirically as halos indicate elevated IOP',
'Arrange MRI brain as halos around lights indicate optic neuritis',
'C',
'CORRECT. Halos around lights have two main causes that must be distinguished: (1) Corneal oedema — from contact lens overwear, early keratoconus, or Fuchs endothelial dystrophy — this patient''s symptoms improving with lens removal strongly suggest contact lens-related corneal oedema; (2) Elevated IOP — raised IOP in glaucoma or acute angle-closure causes corneal oedema by disrupting endothelial pump function. The key investigation is IOP measurement. In primary care: arrange urgent optician assessment for IOP measurement, corneal assessment, and contact lens evaluation. If IOP is elevated or if the clinical picture is uncertain, hospital eye service referral is appropriate. Contact lens-related halos: typically from overwear causing hypoxic corneal oedema — management is optimising lens wear time, switching to higher oxygen-permeable lenses, or using daily disposable lenses. The absence of pain, red eye, and associated symptoms (nausea, vomiting) makes acute angle-closure glaucoma unlikely, but IOP must be checked to exclude it. NICE',
'Incorrect. Halos are not always caused by acute angle-closure glaucoma. The absence of pain, red eye, vomiting, reduced vision, and mid-dilated fixed pupil makes acute angle-closure glaucoma unlikely in this patient. Emergency referral is not warranted when contact lens overwear is the most probable cause, but IOP should be checked.',
'Incorrect. While contact lens-related corneal oedema is the most likely cause given the clinical history, halos can indicate raised IOP and must not be dismissed without IOP measurement. Reassurance without IOP checking risks missing glaucoma in a young patient.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Prescribing anti-glaucoma drops empirically without measuring IOP or confirming a glaucoma diagnosis is inappropriate. IOP may be normal in this patient (contact lens-related corneal oedema) — prescribing latanoprost without a diagnosis would expose the patient to unnecessary treatment (and its side effects) without benefit.',
'Incorrect. Optic neuritis causes unilateral painful visual loss with a central scotoma and reduced colour vision — it does not cause halos around lights. MRI brain is not the appropriate investigation for halos. The correct first step is IOP measurement and contact lens assessment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 58-year-old woman with a known diagnosis of giant cell arteritis (GCA) on high-dose prednisolone 60 mg OD (started 3 weeks ago by the rheumatology team) presents to her GP with sudden onset of complete painless visual loss in her left eye this morning. She woke up unable to see from her left eye. Her CRP last week was 8 mg/L (down from 68 mg/L at diagnosis). Her ESR was 32 mm/hr. She is currently on prednisolone, lansoprazole, alendronate, and calcium/vitamin D. According to NICE guidance on GCA and its ophthalmic complications, which of the following is the most appropriate immediate management?',
'Reassure her that transient visual symptoms are common when starting prednisolone and review in 1 week',
'Increase prednisolone to 80 mg OD and arrange urgent rheumatology review within 48 hours',
'Call 999 for immediate hospital admission — visual loss in a patient on treatment for GCA represents anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (AION) from GCA; IV methylprednisolone 1g daily for 3 days is required to protect the remaining eye; this is a sight-threatening and life-threatening emergency',
'Reduce prednisolone as the falling CRP suggests the GCA is controlled and the visual symptoms are likely steroid side effects',
'Arrange urgent ophthalmology assessment within 2 weeks to assess for ocular hypertension from steroid use',
'C',
'CORRECT. Visual loss in a patient with known GCA — even one on treatment — represents a GCA-related anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy (AION) until proven otherwise. GCA-related AION occurs when inflamed temporal arterial branches compromise the posterior ciliary arteries supplying the optic nerve head, causing ischaemic infarction. This is an ocular emergency because: (1) The involved eye has likely suffered irreversible visual loss; (2) There is a significant risk of involvement of the contralateral eye — the fellow eye can be lost within hours to days; (3) IV methylprednisolone 1g daily for 3 days (pulsed IV steroids) is given urgently to protect the remaining eye by suppressing ongoing vasculitis. The falling CRP/ESR represents a response to oral prednisolone but does not mean the vasculitis has been completely suppressed — breakthrough visual loss on oral prednisolone is a recognised occurrence and mandates IV steroid escalation. Same-day emergency admission is required. NICE',
'Incorrect. Visual loss in GCA is never benign or transient from prednisolone effects. Prednisolone does not cause sudden complete visual loss. This is an ophthalmic emergency — GCA-related AION — and a 1-week review would result in permanent visual loss in the remaining eye.',
'Incorrect. Increasing oral prednisolone to 80 mg is insufficient for a GCA ocular emergency. IV methylprednisolone (pulsed doses of 0.5–1g daily for 3 days) is required to achieve higher drug levels and more rapid suppression of vasculitis. A 48-hour rheumatology review is too slow for this emergency.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Reducing prednisolone in a patient with active GCA visual complications would be catastrophically wrong. The visual loss indicates ongoing vascular inflammation — reducing immunosuppression would accelerate further damage and put both eyes at immediate risk.',
'Incorrect. A 2-week ophthalmology assessment is dangerously too slow. Visual loss in GCA requires same-day emergency hospital admission. Steroid-induced ocular hypertension is a valid long-term concern in a patient on high-dose prednisolone, but it is not the explanation for sudden complete visual loss in a GCA patient — this is AION and constitutes an emergency.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 25-year-old woman presents to her GP with a 2-day history of right eye redness and a small, raised, red nodule on the temporal aspect of the right conjunctiva adjacent to the limbus. The nodule is slightly tender. Her visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. She has no discharge, no photophobia, and no previous eye problems. She is otherwise fit and well with no known systemic conditions. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and the most appropriate management?',
'Bacterial conjunctivitis; prescribe topical chloramphenicol for 5 days',
'Acute angle-closure glaucoma; refer immediately to the emergency eye unit',
'Episcleritis — a benign self-limiting inflammation of the superficial episcleral tissue; reassure the patient; prescribe topical lubricating drops or a short course of topical NSAIDs (ketorolac) for symptomatic relief; most cases resolve within 2–4 weeks',
'Scleritis; refer same-day to the emergency eye unit for systemic investigation',
'Corneal ulcer; prescribe intensive topical fluoroquinolone drops and refer urgently to ophthalmology',
'C',
'CORRECT. Episcleritis is a common, benign, typically self-limiting inflammation of the superficial vascular layer between the conjunctiva and the sclera. It presents as: a localised (or diffuse) salmon-pink or red nodular (or sectoral) injection adjacent to the limbus, mild tenderness, no discharge, normal VA, and minimal discomfort. It is most common in young adults and women. The episcleral injection blanches with topical phenylephrine (distinguishing it from scleritis — scleral vessels do not blanch). Most cases are idiopathic and resolve within 2–4 weeks. Management: reassurance + topical lubricants (artificial tears) for symptomatic relief; topical NSAIDs (ketorolac) can reduce discomfort; oral NSAIDs for more symptomatic cases. Systemic investigation for underlying causes (RA, IBD, gout) may be considered for recurrent or bilateral episcleritis. Urgent referral is not required for straightforward episcleritis. NICE CKS',
'Incorrect. Bacterial conjunctivitis presents with mucopurulent discharge and diffuse conjunctival injection — not a discrete raised nodule at the limbus with no discharge. The presentation here is typical of episcleritis.',
'Incorrect. Acute angle-closure glaucoma presents with severe eye pain, headache, nausea, vomiting, rainbow halos, mid-dilated fixed pupil, and dramatically reduced vision — it is not a small localised nodule with normal VA and no pain. Emergency referral is not indicated for episcleritis.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Scleritis causes a deep boring severe pain (often nocturnal), violaceous (purple) discolouration of the deep scleral vessels (which do not blanch with phenylephrine), and is associated with systemic autoimmune disease. The pain in scleritis is significantly more severe than in episcleritis. This patient has a mildly tender nodule — consistent with nodular episcleritis, not scleritis.',
'Incorrect. A corneal ulcer presents with severe pain, photophobia, reduced visual acuity, and a visible white opacity on the cornea — not a nodular elevation of the conjunctival/episcleral tissue with normal VA and no photophobia. Fluoroquinolone drops are not appropriate for episcleritis.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 68-year-old man attends his GP for a routine review. He has type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and hyperlipidaemia. During the review, he mentions he was seen at the hospital eye service last year and was told he has "non-exudative AMD in both eyes" (dry AMD). He asks his GP whether there are any new treatments available. He has read online about anti-VEGF injections for AMD and asks whether he should be having them. According to current NICE guidance on AMD treatment, which of the following most accurately responds to his question?',
'Anti-VEGF injections (ranibizumab, aflibercept) are approved by NICE for all types of AMD including dry AMD — refer urgently for injection treatment',
'There is no approved disease-modifying treatment for dry AMD; AREDS2 supplements reduce progression risk in intermediate dry AMD; advise him to monitor for symptoms of wet AMD conversion (metamorphopsia, sudden visual loss) and attend urgent ophthalmology if these develop',
'Dry AMD always converts to wet AMD within 5 years — refer urgently for prophylactic anti-VEGF injections to prevent conversion',
'Recommend he increases his intake of carrots and spinach as these contain lutein which will reverse his dry AMD',
'Reassure him that dry AMD has no significant visual consequences and no follow-up is needed',
'B',
'CORRECT. Anti-VEGF agents (ranibizumab, aflibercept, brolucizumab, faricimab) are approved by NICE for wet (neovascular) AMD only — they work by inhibiting VEGF-driven choroidal neovascularisation. They have NO role in dry AMD. There is currently no approved disease-modifying treatment to reverse or halt dry AMD. Evidence-based management of dry AMD: (1) AREDS2 supplements: proven to reduce progression from intermediate to advanced AMD by approximately 25% (lutein 10mg, zeaxanthin 2mg, vitamin C 500mg, vitamin E 400IU, zinc 80mg); (2) Smoking cessation: most important modifiable risk factor; (3) Patient education: recognise symptoms of conversion to wet AMD (metamorphopsia — straight lines appearing wavy — or sudden vision loss) and seek urgent ophthalmology review if these develop; (4) Amsler grid for home monitoring. Emerging treatments for geographic atrophy (pegcetacoplan, avacincaptad pegol — complement inhibitors) are in development but not yet NICE-approved. NICE',
'Incorrect. Anti-VEGF injections are only approved for wet (neovascular) AMD — not dry AMD. Dry AMD does not involve choroidal neovascularisation, so anti-VEGF therapy would be ineffective. Referring a dry AMD patient for anti-VEGF injection treatment would be inappropriate.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Dry AMD does not always convert to wet AMD — approximately 10–15% of dry AMD patients develop wet AMD over their lifetime. Prophylactic anti-VEGF injections are not used to prevent conversion and would expose the patient to unnecessary procedural risks (endophthalmitis, retinal tear, haemorrhage) without proven benefit.',
'Incorrect. While dietary carotenoids (lutein, zeaxanthin) have evidence for reducing AMD progression risk when provided in the AREDS2 supplement formulation, carrots do not contain significant amounts of lutein or zeaxanthin (they contain beta-carotene, which was NOT shown to benefit AMD and may increase lung cancer risk in smokers). Advising dietary carrots as a treatment for AMD is not evidence-based.',
'Incorrect. Dry AMD can progress to geographic atrophy causing significant central vision loss, and can convert to wet AMD causing rapid severe central vision loss. It is not a benign condition without visual consequences. Regular ophthalmology follow-up and patient education about conversion symptoms are important.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 70-year-old woman with hypertension, type 2 diabetes, and atrial fibrillation (on apixaban) presents to her GP with a 1-week history of left eye discomfort and redness. On examination, there is diffuse conjunctival injection in the left eye, a moderate amount of mucopurulent discharge, and her eyelids are stuck together in the morning. Her VA is 6/6 bilaterally. She has no photophobia and the cornea appears clear. Her right eye is unaffected. She has not had this before. Which of the following is the most appropriate management, and which factor in her history is most relevant to safety-netting?',
'Prescribe topical dexamethasone 0.1% eye drops as first-line for conjunctivitis in elderly patients',
'Prescribe topical chloramphenicol 0.5% four times daily for 5 days; safety-net specifically that if VA deteriorates, photophobia develops, or symptoms fail to improve within 48 hours she must return or attend the emergency eye unit urgently — in an elderly patient on anticoagulation, orbital cellulitis spreading from conjunctivitis is a potential but rare serious complication',
'Prescribe oral ciprofloxacin for 7 days as elderly patients require systemic antibiotics for conjunctivitis',
'Refer urgently to ophthalmology as conjunctivitis in patients on anticoagulation requires specialist assessment before any treatment',
'Reassure and advise over-the-counter lubricating drops; bacterial conjunctivitis is self-limiting in elderly patients',
'B',
'CORRECT. This presentation is consistent with bacterial conjunctivitis: unilateral mucopurulent discharge, stuck eyelids, diffuse conjunctival injection, normal VA, no photophobia, no corneal opacity. First-line treatment per NICE CKS: topical chloramphenicol 0.5% four times daily for 5–7 days. Key safety-netting considerations: (1) Return urgently if: vision deteriorates, photophobia develops (suggests corneal involvement — keratitis), increasing pain, swelling of the eyelids or periorbital tissue (orbital/preseptal cellulitis), or failure to improve within 48 hours; (2) In an elderly patient on apixaban, a bacterial conjunctivitis that spreads to cause orbital cellulitis (rare) could be complicated by orbital haemorrhage — this is the most relevant safety consideration in the context of anticoagulation, although it does not change the initial management; (3) Her diabetes also increases infection susceptibility. Topical dexamethasone is contraindicated as first-line — it can worsen bacterial and viral infections and cause secondary glaucoma/cataract with prolonged use. NICE',
'Incorrect. Topical corticosteroids (dexamethasone) are contraindicated as first-line treatment for conjunctivitis — they can worsen bacterial infections (including masking a herpetic keratitis with potentially catastrophic consequences) and cause secondary glaucoma and cataract with prolonged use. They are used under specialist supervision for specific inflammatory eye conditions.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Oral ciprofloxacin is not indicated for bacterial conjunctivitis — topical antibiotics achieve far higher local drug concentrations in the conjunctival sac without systemic side effects. Oral antibiotics for conjunctivitis would be inappropriate and contribute to antibiotic resistance.',
'Incorrect. While conjunctivitis in anticoagulated patients does not require specialist assessment before any treatment, the statement in option D has some merit in that close monitoring is appropriate. However, anticoagulation alone does not contraindicate treating bacterial conjunctivitis with topical chloramphenicol in primary care.',
'Incorrect. While bacterial conjunctivitis is largely self-limiting in immunocompetent young adults (resolving in 7–10 days), the evidence shows that topical antibiotics accelerate resolution and reduce the period of infectivity. In an elderly patient with diabetes and immunosuppression, expectant management alone without topical antibiotics is not optimal practice.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP is advising a 55-year-old man who is about to start long-term amiodarone therapy for atrial fibrillation. The cardiologist has initiated the amiodarone 200 mg OD. The patient asks the GP about potential eye side effects. According to your knowledge of amiodarone ocular toxicity, which of the following most accurately describes the expected ocular findings and the monitoring requirements?',
'Amiodarone causes acute optic neuritis requiring cessation of the drug within the first month of treatment',
'Amiodarone is safe for the eyes with no known ocular side effects',
'Amiodarone causes corneal microdeposits (vortex keratopathy/cornea verticillata) in virtually all patients on long-term therapy — these are benign, do not impair vision significantly, and do not require cessation of amiodarone; rarely, amiodarone can cause optic neuropathy which may cause visual loss and requires specialist assessment',
'Amiodarone causes severe retinopathy similar to hydroxychloroquine retinopathy and requires annual OCT screening from year 1',
'Amiodarone eye drops are required to prevent the systemic drug from depositing in the cornea',
'C',
'CORRECT. Amiodarone ocular side effects: (1) Corneal microdeposits (vortex keratopathy/cornea verticillata): present in virtually all patients (>90%) taking amiodarone long-term — fine yellowish-brown deposits in the inferior corneal epithelium in a whorl pattern, visible on slit-lamp examination. They cause minimal visual disturbance (occasionally a mild glare or halo around lights) and are NOT an indication to stop amiodarone — they are dose-dependent, reversible on stopping, and benign; (2) Amiodarone optic neuropathy: a rare but serious complication causing progressive or acute visual loss from optic nerve toxicity — this requires cessation of amiodarone and urgent ophthalmology assessment. Unlike hydroxychloroquine, amiodarone does not cause retinopathy. Patients should be advised to report any visual deterioration and have baseline ophthalmology assessment before starting or early in treatment. NICE/BNF',
'Incorrect. Amiodarone does not cause acute optic neuritis (an inflammatory demyelinating condition) — it causes a toxic optic neuropathy, which is different in mechanism and presentation. Furthermore, this would not typically occur within the first month and would be a reason to stop amiodarone, not a routine expected finding.',
'Incorrect. Amiodarone has well-recognised ocular side effects — corneal microdeposits in virtually all patients on long-term therapy and, rarely, optic neuropathy. Claiming it is safe for the eyes with no known ocular side effects is factually incorrect.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Amiodarone does not cause hydroxychloroquine-type macular/retinal toxicity — the ocular toxicity profiles are different. Amiodarone causes corneal deposits and rarely optic neuropathy. Annual OCT retinal screening is not part of the monitoring protocol for amiodarone (it is for hydroxychloroquine).',
'Incorrect. Amiodarone eye drops do not exist as a preventive measure. The corneal deposits from amiodarone accumulate from the drug in the lacrimal fluid and tears — there is no topical intervention that prevents this. Corneal deposits with systemic amiodarone are an expected pharmacological effect, not a complication that can be prevented with drops.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 66-year-old woman presents to her GP worried about her vision. She reports that she had a routine optician appointment last week and was told she has "early macular changes" in both eyes. She has no visual symptoms. Her optician did not explain what this means. She is a current smoker (20 cigarettes/day, 40 pack-years), has hypertension controlled on amlodipine, and a BMI of 31 kg/m². She has no family history of eye disease. According to NICE guidance on AMD risk factors and primary care management, which of the following is the most important modifiable risk factor to address at this appointment?',
'Prescribe glasses for her AMD as optical correction prevents progression',
'Optimise her blood pressure as hypertension is the strongest risk factor for AMD progression',
'Advise smoking cessation — smoking is the single strongest modifiable risk factor for AMD, approximately doubling the risk; in addition, advise on AREDS2 supplements if she has intermediate AMD and arrange follow-up ophthalmology review of the optician findings',
'Prescribe oral simvastatin as statin therapy has been shown to reverse AMD in population studies',
'Reassure her that "early macular changes" is not AMD and no lifestyle modification is required',
'C',
'CORRECT. Smoking is the single most important modifiable risk factor for age-related macular degeneration (AMD) — it approximately doubles the risk of developing AMD and significantly accelerates progression from early to advanced AMD. The mechanism involves: oxidative damage to photoreceptors and retinal pigment epithelium, choroidal vasoconstriction reducing oxygen delivery, and increased vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) levels. Primary care priorities for this patient: (1) Smoking cessation — most important intervention; offer NRT/varenicline, refer to NHS Stop Smoking Service; (2) If the optician findings represent intermediate AMD (multiple soft drusen): AREDS2 supplements (lutein/zeaxanthin formula) reduce 5-year progression risk by 25%; (3) Blood pressure optimisation — hypertension is a risk factor but less strongly than smoking; (4) UV protection (sunglasses); (5) Clarify the optician''s findings — arrange ophthalmology review to characterise the macular changes accurately. NICE',
'Incorrect. Glasses do not prevent AMD progression — AMD is caused by retinal degeneration, not refractive error. Optical correction has no role in AMD management.',
'Incorrect. While hypertension is a risk factor for AMD (and cardiovascular disease in general), smoking is the SINGLE STRONGEST modifiable risk factor for AMD. Blood pressure control is important but not the most important risk factor to address at this appointment when the patient is a 40 pack-year current smoker.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. There is no evidence that statins reverse AMD. While some observational studies suggested statin use might be associated with lower AMD risk (potentially through anti-inflammatory and anti-VEGF effects), prescribing statins specifically for AMD is not recommended by NICE. Statins are prescribed for cardiovascular risk reduction.',
'Incorrect. "Early macular changes" at the optician may represent early AMD (drusen) which, while currently asymptomatic, can progress. It should not be dismissed. Smoking cessation, AREDS2 supplements (if intermediate AMD is confirmed), and ophthalmology review for accurate characterisation of the findings are all appropriate.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 45-year-old woman with known HIV (CD4 count 38 cells/µL, not yet on ART) presents to the GP with a 2-week history of right-sided visual loss, floaters, and flashing lights. She has no eye pain. On fundoscopy, the GP notes areas of white retinal necrosis with haemorrhage in a "pizza-pie" or "ketchup and cheese" pattern along the retinal vessels. There is no anterior segment inflammation. According to your knowledge of opportunistic infections in HIV, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and the most appropriate management?',
'Diabetic maculopathy — refer routinely to ophthalmology and optimise glycaemic control',
'Toxoplasmosis retinitis — prescribe pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine and refer to HIV medicine',
'Cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis — this is an AIDS-defining illness in severe immunodeficiency; urgent same-day ophthalmology assessment is required; systemic treatment with IV ganciclovir or oral valganciclovir should be initiated urgently; ART must be started without delay',
'Herpes simplex virus acute retinal necrosis — prescribe high-dose oral aciclovir and refer urgently',
'Choroidal tuberculosis — arrange QuantiFERON-TB Gold and chest X-ray before any treatment',
'C',
'CORRECT. CMV retinitis is the most common serious opportunistic ocular infection in advanced HIV disease (CD4 <50 cells/µL) and was a major cause of blindness before effective antiretroviral therapy. Classic fundoscopic appearance: "pizza-pie" or "cottage cheese and ketchup" — areas of white necrotic retina (cottage cheese) with haemorrhage (ketchup) spreading along retinal vessels. Features: painless, no significant anterior uveitis (helps distinguish from herpetic retinitis), progressive if untreated. Management: (1) Urgent same-day ophthalmology assessment; (2) Systemic anti-CMV treatment: oral valganciclovir (preferred for mild-moderate CMV retinitis) or IV ganciclovir (for severe sight-threatening disease or systemic CMV); (3) Urgent ART initiation — immune reconstitution reduces CMV progression and is critical for long-term management; (4) CMV retinitis is an AIDS-defining illness — notifiable and triggers ART regardless of any planned timing. NICE',
'Incorrect. Diabetic maculopathy presents in the context of known diabetes and shows hard exudates, microaneurysms, and haemorrhages in a macular distribution — not the necrotising white retinal lesions with haemorrhage along vessels seen in CMV retinitis. This patient has no mention of diabetes.',
'Incorrect. Toxoplasma retinochoroiditis (ocular toxoplasmosis) typically presents as a focal white chorioretinal lesion adjacent to an old pigmented scar ("headlight in the fog" appearance) with significant vitritis (hazy vitreous). It causes significant anterior and vitreous inflammation unlike CMV retinitis. Treatment is pyrimethamine + sulfadiazine + folinic acid, but this is not the diagnosis here.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Acute retinal necrosis (ARN) from herpes viruses (VZV, HSV) presents with rapid-onset peripheral necrotising retinitis with significant vitritis, anterior uveitis, and often arteritis — it is more painful and inflammatory than CMV retinitis. The "pizza-pie" pattern along vessels is specific to CMV. ARN treatment is IV aciclovir/ganciclovir — but this is not the diagnosis here.',
'Incorrect. Choroidal tuberculosis is a rare manifestation of disseminated TB presenting as choroidal granulomata — discrete yellowish-white lesions in the choroid. It would not present as the necrotising retinal lesions with haemorrhage pattern described here. While TB testing is part of the HIV workup, it is not the first management priority for this presentation.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A GP is teaching a medical student about the red eye in primary care. The student asks how to differentiate between the causes of red eye. The GP presents 5 different clinical scenarios. Which of the following red eye presentations is the ODD ONE OUT — i.e., which one should always be managed in primary care without requiring emergency ophthalmology referral?',
'A 25-year-old contact lens wearer with a painful red eye, photophobia, and a white corneal opacity — hypopyon present',
'A 70-year-old with sudden severe eye pain, vomiting, rock-hard eye, haloes around lights, and a mid-dilated fixed pupil',
'A 45-year-old with a unilateral red eye, deep boring nocturnal pain, violaceous discolouration, and reduced visual acuity',
'A 30-year-old with bilateral itchy, watery, red eyes, no discharge, seasonal symptoms, and normal visual acuity',
'A 22-year-old with a painful red eye, photophobia, irregular pupil, and circumcorneal flush — known to have ankylosing spondylitis',
'D',
'CORRECT — ODD ONE OUT. Option D (bilateral allergic conjunctivitis) is the only presentation that should be managed in primary care without emergency ophthalmology referral. Management: topical mast cell stabilisers (sodium cromoglicate), topical/oral antihistamines, avoidance of allergens. All other options require emergency same-day ophthalmology referral: (A) Bacterial keratitis with hypopyon — sight-threatening emergency; (B) Acute angle-closure glaucoma — ophthalmic emergency with IOP typically >50 mmHg; (C) Scleritis — sight-threatening, associated with systemic autoimmune disease; (E) Acute anterior uveitis with HLA-B27 association — requires topical steroids and cycloplegics under specialist supervision. The key distinguishing features of allergic conjunctivitis: bilateral, ITCHING (the cardinal symptom of allergy), watery discharge, seasonal pattern, no pain, no photophobia, normal visual acuity, and association with other atopic conditions. NICE',
'Incorrect as the odd one out. A painful red eye with corneal opacity, hypopyon, and photophobia in a contact lens wearer is bacterial keratitis — a sight-threatening emergency requiring same-day ophthalmology referral for intensive topical fluoroquinolone therapy. This DOES require emergency referral.',
'Incorrect as the odd one out. Sudden severe eye pain, vomiting, rock-hard eye, haloes, and mid-dilated fixed pupil is acute angle-closure glaucoma — an ophthalmic emergency with IOP >50 mmHg causing ischaemic optic nerve damage. This DOES require immediate emergency referral and treatment.',
'Incorrect as the odd one out. Deep boring nocturnal pain, violaceous discolouration, and reduced VA is scleritis — associated with systemic autoimmune diseases and requiring same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment. This DOES require emergency referral.',
'CORRECT — see above. Bilateral itchy watery eyes with seasonal symptoms is allergic conjunctivitis — the only one safely managed in primary care.',
'Incorrect as the odd one out. Acute anterior uveitis (circumcorneal flush, irregular pupil, photophobia, pain in a patient with ankylosing spondylitis) requires same-day emergency ophthalmology assessment for topical steroids and cycloplegics. This DOES require emergency referral.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 58-year-old woman on long-term prednisolone 10 mg OD for polymyalgia rheumatica (for 4 years) attends her GP for a medication review. She has no visual symptoms. Her GP is reviewing the ophthalmological complications of long-term corticosteroid use. According to your knowledge of steroid-induced ocular complications, which of the following most accurately describes the complications she should be screened for?',
'Long-term oral corticosteroids do not cause ocular complications at doses below 20 mg OD',
'Long-term corticosteroid use is associated with posterior subcapsular cataract and steroid-induced glaucoma (raised IOP); she should be referred to optometry or ophthalmology for regular IOP checks and lens assessment',
'Long-term oral prednisolone causes corneal thinning requiring specialist corneal assessment annually',
'Long-term corticosteroids only cause ocular complications via the topical route — systemic steroids have no ocular side effects',
'Steroid-induced ocular complications only occur with inhaled corticosteroids, not oral prednisolone',
'B',
'CORRECT. Long-term systemic corticosteroid use is associated with two main ocular complications: (1) Posterior subcapsular cataract (PSC): a characteristic lens opacity forming at the posterior pole of the lens, causing glare, photophobia, and reduced vision in bright light — the risk increases with cumulative dose and duration of corticosteroid therapy; PSC is more visually disabling than the nuclear or anterior cortical cataracts of normal ageing because of its central location; (2) Steroid-induced glaucoma (ocular hypertension): systemic corticosteroids increase IOP in approximately 5% of the general population (steroid responders) by reducing trabecular meshwork outflow; undetected raised IOP causes progressive optic nerve damage. Management: annual eye test with IOP measurement via optometry or ophthalmology. Patients on long-term systemic corticosteroids should be advised to report any visual changes and should have regular ophthalmological monitoring. NICE/BNF',
'Incorrect. Long-term oral corticosteroids cause ocular complications regardless of dose — even low doses (prednisolone 5–10 mg OD) carry risk of posterior subcapsular cataract and steroid-induced glaucoma with prolonged use. There is no safe dose threshold below which ocular monitoring is unnecessary.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Corneal thinning is not a recognised complication of systemic oral corticosteroid use. Topical corticosteroid eye drops used long-term can affect corneal structure and cause secondary infections (herpes simplex reactivation), but systemic prednisolone does not primarily cause corneal thinning.',
'Incorrect. Both systemic (oral) and topical corticosteroids cause ocular complications. Systemic corticosteroids cause posterior subcapsular cataract and steroid-induced glaucoma through systemic distribution to ocular tissues. Topical corticosteroid eye drops additionally carry risks of corneal infection (HSV) and corneal ulceration. The claim that systemic steroids have no ocular effects is factually incorrect.',
'Incorrect. Both inhaled and oral corticosteroids can cause ocular complications. There is evidence that even inhaled corticosteroids (particularly at high doses) increase the risk of posterior subcapsular cataract and glaucoma — though the risk is higher with systemic oral administration. Oral prednisolone at any dose used long-term carries ocular risk.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 76-year-old woman is reviewed by her GP following a recent fall at home. She tripped on a rug and sustained a minor forearm injury. She lives alone and her son is concerned about her safety. Her visual acuity has been declining gradually. On review, her current visual acuity is 6/36 in the right eye (she was 6/18 last year) and 6/60 in the left eye. She has known bilateral wet AMD and has been receiving intravitreal anti-VEGF injections at the hospital eye service. She has not attended her last two injection appointments. According to NICE guidance on falls prevention and visual impairment in older adults, which of the following most accurately describes the most appropriate primary care response?',
'Reassure her son that visual impairment is expected in AMD and falls are an inevitable consequence',
'Contact the hospital eye service to re-establish her missed intravitreal injection appointments urgently as delays in anti-VEGF treatment cause irreversible AMD progression; arrange a falls risk assessment; consider referral to falls prevention services and a home safety assessment; discuss whether she needs a carer or assistive devices for visual impairment',
'Prescribe oral vitamin A supplements to improve her visual acuity before re-engaging with the hospital eye service',
'Advise her to move to a nursing home as visual impairment causing falls indicates she can no longer safely live alone',
'Arrange ophthalmology assessment in 6 weeks as the missed injection appointments are not urgent',
'B',
'CORRECT. This patient has multiple compounding problems requiring a comprehensive primary care response: (1) Wet AMD with missed anti-VEGF treatment: missed intravitreal injections in wet AMD cause irreversible retinal damage — each missed appointment risks further choroidal neovascularisation growth and permanent vision loss. The GP should urgently contact the hospital eye service to re-establish her treatment and explain the urgency; (2) Falls risk: visual impairment is one of the strongest modifiable risk factors for falls in older adults — NICE guidance on falls prevention recommends a comprehensive falls risk assessment; (3) Home safety: referral to occupational therapy for home hazard assessment (rug removal, improved lighting, grab rails); (4) Social assessment: a lone elderly woman with significant bilateral visual impairment may need social care input — carer, visual impairment rehabilitation, registration with RNIB; (5) Registration as visually impaired: visual acuity 6/60 in the better eye may qualify for Certificate of Visual Impairment (CVI) as severely sight impaired. NICE',
'Incorrect. Reassuring the son that falls are inevitable is inappropriate and inaccurate. Multiple modifiable factors contribute to fall risk in this patient — visual impairment from missed AMD treatment is treatable, and falls prevention interventions have strong evidence. Accepting falls as inevitable without addressing the treatable cause is inadequate care.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Vitamin A supplements do not treat or improve wet AMD. Wet AMD requires anti-VEGF injections to control choroidal neovascularisation — nothing else. Vitamin A supplementation has no role in AMD management (and excess vitamin A can cause toxicity including intracranial hypertension).',
'Incorrect. Advising a nursing home as the first response to visual impairment is disproportionate and premature. The appropriate response is to re-establish her AMD treatment (potentially improving her vision), arrange falls prevention and home safety assessment, and consider social care support to enable her to continue living safely at home if possible. Patient autonomy and least-restrictive options must be considered.',
'Incorrect. A 6-week delay to reassess missed intravitreal injections is entirely inappropriate. Wet AMD can progress rapidly — each missed monthly injection can result in significant permanent visual loss. The GP should urgently contact the hospital eye service to expedite re-engagement with treatment.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 50-year-old woman presents to her GP with bilateral watering eyes (epiphora) that have been present for 2 years. She has no discharge, no eye pain, and no visual symptoms. She has tried lubricating drops without benefit. On examination, both eyes appear normal with no corneal abnormality. The GP gently presses the lacrimal sac area (below the medial canthus) and a small amount of mucopurulent material regurgitates from the punctum. Which of the following most accurately identifies the diagnosis and the appropriate management?',
'This is blepharitis; prescribe warm compresses and lid massage',
'This is chronic dacryocystitis from nasolacrimal duct obstruction; refer to oculoplastics for dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) assessment; topical or oral antibiotics may be used for infective episodes',
'This is allergic conjunctivitis; prescribe topical sodium cromoglicate',
'This is dry eye disease; prescribe hypromellose eye drops four times daily',
'This is conjunctivochalasis; refer for conjunctival surgery',
'B',
'CORRECT. The clinical finding of mucopurulent material regurgitating from the punctum on pressure over the lacrimal sac (regurgitation test positive) is diagnostic of chronic dacryocystitis from nasolacrimal duct (NLD) obstruction. The mechanism: NLD obstruction → stasis of tears and debris in the lacrimal sac → secondary bacterial colonisation → chronic dacryocystitis. Symptoms: chronic watering eye (epiphora), recurrent discharge or matted lashes. Complications: acute dacryocystitis (painful preseptal/orbital cellulitis from infected lacrimal sac) — presenting with a hot tender swelling below the medial canthus. Management: (1) Refer to oculoplastic surgery for dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) — surgical creation of a new drainage channel bypassing the obstructed NLD (between the lacrimal sac and nasal mucosa); (2) Topical antibiotics (chloramphenicol) or oral antibiotics (co-amoxiclav) for acute infective episodes; (3) Warm compresses for symptomatic relief. NICE',
'Incorrect. Blepharitis causes lid margin inflammation with crusting, greasy scales, and madarosis — it can cause secondary epiphora from tear film instability and punctal inflammation. However, the positive regurgitation test (mucopurulent material from the punctum) specifically indicates nasolacrimal duct obstruction and dacryocystitis, not blepharitis alone.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. Allergic conjunctivitis causes bilateral itchy, watery, red eyes with no discharge — it does not cause mucopurulent regurgitation from the punctum on lacrimal sac massage. There is no itching described and the watering is the presenting complaint without allergic features.',
'Incorrect. Dry eye disease causes symptoms of gritty, foreign body sensation, burning, and paradoxical reflex tearing — not mucopurulent material from the lacrimal punctum. Dry eye drops would not address nasolacrimal duct obstruction.',
'Incorrect. Conjunctivochalasis (lax, redundant conjunctival folds) can cause epiphora by interfering with the tear meniscus and lacrimal punctum drainage — but it would not cause mucopurulent material to regurgitate from the punctum on lacrimal sac massage. The positive regurgitation test is specific for NLD obstruction with dacryocystitis.',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0),

(1,'Clinical Medicine','Ophthalmology',NULL,'Hard',
'A 60-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes presents to her GP with a 3-day history of right eyelid swelling, pain, and redness. She noticed it started with what she thought was a stye. On examination, her right upper and lower eyelids are oedematous and erythematous. The swelling appears to be anterior to the orbital septum (the eyelid skin is warm and red, but on asking the patient to look in all directions, her eye movements are full and painless). Her visual acuity is 6/6 bilaterally. Her temperature is 37.8°C and she feels generally unwell. Which of the following most accurately distinguishes the diagnosis and the most appropriate management?',
'This is a severe stye — prescribe topical chloramphenicol and warm compresses and review in 5 days',
'This is preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis — treat with oral co-amoxiclav 625 mg TDS for 7 days; review in 48 hours; if no improvement or if she develops any orbital signs (proptosis, restricted painful eye movements, ophthalmoplegia, reduced VA), admit immediately for IV antibiotics as this may represent orbital cellulitis',
'This is orbital cellulitis — call 999 for immediate hospital admission for IV antibiotics and CT orbits',
'This is allergic periorbital oedema — prescribe oral antihistamines and topical hydrocortisone cream',
'This is chalazion with secondary infection — perform incision and curettage in the GP surgery',
'B',
'CORRECT. The key distinction here is between preseptal (periorbital) cellulitis and orbital cellulitis: Preseptal cellulitis: infection anterior to the orbital septum — eyelid erythema and oedema, full and PAINLESS eye movements, normal visual acuity, no proptosis. This is what this patient has. Orbital cellulitis: infection posterior to the orbital septum — in addition to lid swelling, there is proptosis, PAINFUL or RESTRICTED eye movements, chemosis (conjunctival swelling), and reduced visual acuity (from optic nerve involvement). Orbital cellulitis is a life-threatening emergency requiring immediate hospital admission for IV antibiotics and CT orbits to assess for subperiosteal abscess. Management for preseptal cellulitis: oral co-amoxiclav (first-line for the most common organisms — Staph aureus, Strep pyogenes) for 7 days; in diabetics, close monitoring is essential as infection can progress rapidly to orbital cellulitis. Safety-netting is critical. NICE/BSOP guidelines',
'Incorrect. This presentation (warm erythematous swollen eyelids with full painless eye movements, normal VA) is not a simple stye — it is preseptal cellulitis. Topical chloramphenicol alone is insufficient for preseptal cellulitis, which requires systemic antibiotics. A 5-day review without safety-netting for orbital extension would be inappropriate.',
'Correct — see above.',
'Incorrect. The clinical features — warm, erythematous, oedematous eyelids, fever, and systemic malaise after a stye — indicate bacterial cellulitis, not orbital cellulitis at this stage. Full and painless eye movements distinguish this from orbital cellulitis. Calling 999 for immediate admission would be appropriate IF orbital signs (proptosis, ophthalmoplegia, chemosis, reduced VA) were present — which they are not.',
'Incorrect. Allergic periorbital oedema causes bilateral non-erythematous swelling (oedema without warmth or erythema), associated with urticaria, and typically resolves rapidly. This patient has unilateral warm erythematous swelling with fever and systemic malaise after a stye — this is infective, not allergic.',
'Incorrect. Incision and curettage is the treatment for a chalazion (meibomian cyst) — a non-infective granulomatous lipogranuloma that is non-tender and does not cause fever. Performing I&C on a preseptal cellulitis in the GP surgery would be inappropriate and potentially dangerous (risk of spreading infection).',
NULL,NULL,'active',0,0,0,0);

